WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:03.850
- Super Bowl ads.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:05.223
One final point, Gary.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:07.093
- Buy them.

00:00:07.093 --> 00:00:08.036
(audience laughs)

00:00:08.036 --> 00:00:10.210
(electronic music)

00:00:10.210 --> 00:00:11.963
You got your perspective.

00:00:11.963 --> 00:00:14.367
(audience applauding)

00:00:14.367 --> 00:00:15.920
I just wanna be happy.

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.270
Don't you want to be happy?

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.670
What's up guys, this is a fun conversation

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:27.510
we had the day after the
Super Bowl in New York City

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.210
with some great people from
Pepsi and Knotch and NBCU.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:33.920
Super excited about the conversation.

00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:36.336
Very macro-branding TV stuff

00:00:36.336 --> 00:00:38.080
that I think is a different
look for some of you,

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:39.360
but there's some thematics in it

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.200
that can be unbelievably valuable.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.850
So, some thoughtful
branding and marketing talk.

00:00:43.850 --> 00:00:44.763
Hope you enjoy it.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.120
- Despite any controversies,

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:51.870
advertisers are flocking to the Super Bowl

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.610
to get their brand messages across.

00:00:54.610 --> 00:00:59.306
It's reported that CBS made $382 million

00:00:59.306 --> 00:01:03.040
at five million per 30 second ad,

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:07.330
and it's not surprising because
three out of four people

00:01:07.330 --> 00:01:09.490
say they look forward
to the Super Bowl ads,

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:13.060
and research shows that memorability

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:14.960
for brands in the Super Bowl

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:19.680
are 54% higher than
regular television norms.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.810
So, it's really a great place
to put your ads regardless,

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:27.080
and we're gonna talk a
little bit about that today.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.030
You know, people are
talking about them still,

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.780
they're watching them on YouTube,

00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:34.360
and so, we want to talk
about what is the impact

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:37.340
that the Super Bowl has for ads today

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.930
using some of the Knotch
research measured these ads

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:43.060
and the positive impact

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:45.387
or negative impact that
people felt about them.

00:01:45.387 --> 00:01:50.387
So, the first question
I'm gonna ask is to start,

00:01:50.630 --> 00:01:52.233
maybe you guys can guess,

00:01:52.233 --> 00:01:57.233
3,000 people in our
industry ranked the ads,

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:00.403
can anyone guess what
was the number one ad?

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:04.660
- Don't be shy, it's okay.

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:09.540
- Out of those 12 or all the ones that--

00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:10.990
- Out of these, out of these.
- Out of these 11.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.090
- Do you have a guess?

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.950
- So it was Amazon, Not
Everything Makes the Cut,

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:17.770
and next up was the Gillette,

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.430
We Believe The Best Men Can Be.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:24.070
As a matter of fact, all the ads measured

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:29.070
mostly had positive impact and sentiment,

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:34.160
except for neutral was
Pepsi, More Than Okay,

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.810
Bumble, and Bon &amp; Viv,
The Pitch, of that--

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:40.640
- That was actually the
only one that was negative.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:41.473
- That was negative?

00:02:41.473 --> 00:02:42.306
Oh I'm sorry.

00:02:42.306 --> 00:02:43.139
That was the only one

00:02:43.139 --> 00:02:43.972
that was negative.
- The last one

00:02:43.972 --> 00:02:45.660
that we just saw, the mermaids.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:47.300
I actually just realized,

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.630
this is like the third or
fourth time I've seen it

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.370
and I just got the Shark Tank thing.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.717
I was like ugh.

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.080
I was so confused.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:56.933
Anyways, sorry.

00:02:58.630 --> 00:03:01.480
- Okay but why do you think,

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:03.240
and maybe Anda you can start

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:07.690
that the Amazon and
Gillette ranked so highly?

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.690
- Yeah well I think just at a high level

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.570
this is from a personal standpoint as well

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:13.900
as someone who watches a lot of the ads

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.710
and less of the football.

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:17.470
I thought this year was really interesting

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:20.290
because it felt like most of
the brands played it safe.

00:03:20.290 --> 00:03:22.410
There was a lot of neutrality actually

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:23.710
in the sentiment that we captured

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:26.590
even though it was probably
skewing towards positive.

00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:28.720
I think last year we
saw a bunch of back lash

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:31.410
against some of the advertising
that was put out there.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:35.140
Some of the brands were trying
to take a bolder statement

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:36.910
using cultural

00:03:39.944 --> 00:03:40.777
trends

00:03:40.777 --> 00:03:43.027
and borrowing from the past

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:48.530
of different really great
people in American history.

00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:50.520
And therefore sort of got in trouble

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:51.760
on social shortly after.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:55.210
So I think this year we
saw a lot of the brands

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:56.690
playing it relatively safe.

00:03:56.690 --> 00:03:58.700
Gillette I don't think was
actually on the Super Bowl

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:01.060
but as you all, I'm sure
almost everyone here

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.200
has heard about the ad before
even walking into this room.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.130
And I think really what's going on there

00:04:06.130 --> 00:04:10.110
is obviously tapping
into a massive revolution

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.023
that has happened over the last year.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.320
I've heard very polarizing opinions

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:16.750
from different categories of people.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.570
I think women have had
more positive reactions

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:21.560
than men on average.

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.660
I think it also depends
who Gillette was trying

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.200
to kind of aim towards.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:29.250
Was it men of a particular type of belief,

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:32.090
men who kind of live in urban areas

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:33.480
and have already been touched

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.490
by a lot of the changes

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.320
that have come with the Me Too Movement?

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:38.350
Or was it a very different
category of people?

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:41.160
So I think a lot of these
questions actually pop up for me.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.410
I'm kind of the token
data nerd on the panel

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.627
and I think when we talk
about the Super Bowl

00:04:45.627 --> 00:04:48.740
what I've noticed is brands don't love

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:50.100
hearing about data and measurement.

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.940
Everyone wants to have
a very strong opinion

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:54.200
when it comes to whether it works or not.

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.540
And I think the bigger question for me

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:57.020
which I hope we can tap into tonight

00:04:57.020 --> 00:05:00.560
is to figure out what brands
does it make sense for?

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:02.770
What type of campaigns
does it make sense for?

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:03.920
Is it a brand awareness?

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:05.400
Is it a reach goal?

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.750
And then how do you really
kind of follow up that

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:11.090
on all the other platforms
that matter as well?

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.650
Which all the digital platforms.

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:14.850
How do you make sure
that you're creating more

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.730
than just this one moment in time?

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:19.410
And I'll give you just a quick example

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.030
which I was recently approved to use.

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:23.970
I was having a conversation
with the CM of Denny's.

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:26.178
They did a Super Bowl ad a few years ago

00:05:26.178 --> 00:05:29.210
and they were offering some type of offer.

00:05:29.210 --> 00:05:30.210
They managed actually

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.370
to bring a ton of people into Denny's

00:05:32.370 --> 00:05:33.610
as a follow-up to the Super Bowl.

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.440
So there was a very significant
impact that they saw.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:38.460
And they were able to
deliver on the promise

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:40.057
that they made in the Super Bowl ad

00:05:40.057 --> 00:05:42.300
but then every time the
same group of people

00:05:42.300 --> 00:05:43.520
would go back to Denny's,

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.840
they would get a neutral
to negative experience

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.952
because they actually
haven't figured out by then

00:05:48.952 --> 00:05:52.290
how to scale the rest of the company

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.750
to the traffic and the promise

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.310
that the Super Bowl ad made.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:05:58.740
So I thought that was a
really interesting example

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.880
of this is not just a once in a lifetime

00:06:01.880 --> 00:06:03.690
or once a year type of thing to do.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.040
You actually have to be very thoughtful

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:05.880
about how you use it

00:06:05.880 --> 00:06:07.040
and how it then integrates

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:08.530
not just with the rest of your marketing

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.940
but with the rest of your business?

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:12.360
- So that's an interesting point

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:14.133
because as I was reading some insights

00:06:14.133 --> 00:06:16.160
that journalists were writing about

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.631
from the Super Bowl advertising,

00:06:17.631 --> 00:06:19.330
one thing that came out,

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:21.950
and Gary I'm gonna talk
to you about this one

00:06:21.950 --> 00:06:25.761
is that many marketers
are using social platforms

00:06:25.761 --> 00:06:28.017
to extend the Super Bowl ad.

00:06:28.017 --> 00:06:31.098
And a lot of advertisers
prelaunch the commercials

00:06:31.098 --> 00:06:32.200
to build up.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.260
One example that I thought
was really interesting

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:38.134
was that the avocado growers from Mexico,

00:06:38.134 --> 00:06:41.950
they went to social to
actually make guacamole

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:45.540
like a Super Bowl item that you serve.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:50.540
And they sold 58.4 million avocados

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:53.980
for the Super Bowl this year.

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:57.780
And that was before they even
advertised in the Super Bowl.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:01.510
So I mean this is a huge
change than 10 years ago

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:02.820
and if you could talk a little bit about,

00:07:02.820 --> 00:07:05.300
I mean I know you did a
lot of work with planters

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.760
and social impact before and after.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:08.920
If you could talk a little bit about

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:10.560
how you think it's changed

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:13.380
and what marketers can do to use social

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:17.904
to really extend the life of
their Super Bowl investment?

00:07:17.904 --> 00:07:19.840
- Thanks, thanks for having us.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.650
So from my standpoint it's like

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.585
why wouldn't you use tools

00:07:24.585 --> 00:07:27.660
that are available to you
to drive your business?

00:07:27.660 --> 00:07:31.150
So social to me is a slang term

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:32.450
for the current state

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:35.092
of where people spend
their time on the internet

00:07:35.092 --> 00:07:37.540
which is all about attention.

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:39.648
What's the purpose of these ads?

00:07:39.648 --> 00:07:43.260
Awareness or attention is,

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:45.240
that's the best part about the Super Bowl.

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:47.330
You're gonna get consumption.

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.510
To me, it's why it's my
favorite piece of advertising

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:51.780
in the world in the US, excuse me.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:53.380
It is going to get consumed,

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:55.080
whether on YouTube the week before,

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:56.180
whether during the game,

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:57.590
whether the next day,

00:07:57.590 --> 00:07:59.000
this is going to get consumed.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:07:59.970
So that's exciting.

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:03.350
And then to not use social,

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:04.183
as a matter of fact,

00:08:04.183 --> 00:08:06.250
I actually think brands
should really consider

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:08.030
not showing it on YouTube.

00:08:08.030 --> 00:08:10.310
For five years I was all
about showing it on YouTube

00:08:10.310 --> 00:08:12.220
and now I'm wondering if for next year,

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:13.220
maybe they shouldn't

00:08:13.220 --> 00:08:15.260
because some of the surprise elements

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.020
are being eliminated during the game

00:08:17.020 --> 00:08:18.320
and I'm just kind of like feeling

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:20.890
like is this the tipping point

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:22.180
where you want to pull back?

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.240
But it all leads to the biggest punch line

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:25.930
which is these are businesses

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:27.336
that are trying to drive business.

00:08:27.336 --> 00:08:31.870
And this is one of the
opportunities at their disposal.

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:34.250
But so is Twitter the week before

00:08:34.250 --> 00:08:36.730
and so is pre-roll YouTube the day of.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.110
And so I'm fascinated

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:41.750
by people's binary
points of view on things.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:44.370
It's obviously a mix of a
lot of different things.

00:08:44.370 --> 00:08:46.070
But this is a cultural event.

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:48.440
We're having a business event around it.

00:08:48.440 --> 00:08:51.180
We don't do that for the
Grammy's or the Oscar's

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:52.620
or the NBA finals.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:55.150
So to me, what do I think about it?

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:59.660
I think anybody who doesn't
have a robust strategy

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:02.030
to do creative and media amplification

00:09:02.030 --> 00:09:05.300
on the platforms that
dominate our mobile devices

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.693
is in outer space in 2019.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.340
- So you mentioned creative

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:14.960
and yet, in this room,
I'm sure so many people

00:09:14.960 --> 00:09:17.320
created ads that they're so proud of

00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:19.940
yet there's a lot of ads in the Super Bowl

00:09:19.940 --> 00:09:22.508
like I'm not sure if
anyone saw the Chunky Milk

00:09:22.508 --> 00:09:24.313
by Mint Mobile.

00:09:25.330 --> 00:09:28.570
My family was so horrified and grossed out

00:09:28.570 --> 00:09:29.403
and just thought

00:09:29.403 --> 00:09:31.230
that was the worst
commercial they ever saw.

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:33.940
Yet people are talking about Mint Mobile.

00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:38.937
So does creative really
matter for the Super Bowl?

00:09:41.400 --> 00:09:44.750
- Creative is the variable
of success, the end.

00:09:44.750 --> 00:09:45.970
So yes it does.

00:09:45.970 --> 00:09:47.806
The bigger question becomes

00:09:47.806 --> 00:09:50.470
does everybody talking about it

00:09:50.470 --> 00:09:52.857
mean people are gonna go buy it?

00:09:52.857 --> 00:09:55.160
The industry coming from the outside

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:59.730
of growing up in retail
and SMB and investing

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:00.776
and now coming to it,

00:10:00.776 --> 00:10:05.776
talking about it or winning
these awards or the rankings,

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:09.090
to me somebody within all these companies

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.540
actually cares about the business.

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:15.760
And so I think yes, creative
is the variable of success.

00:10:15.760 --> 00:10:16.820
Did it inspire

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:19.060
or make you think that
you want to consider

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:20.853
and then buy these products?

00:10:21.694 --> 00:10:23.520
A lot of people know

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:26.340
that I have a big passion
for volume of content,

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:30.380
quantity which our industry
believes means not quality

00:10:30.380 --> 00:10:31.936
which I think is silly.

00:10:31.936 --> 00:10:35.245
But I, for the whole decade
that I've been in this industry,

00:10:35.245 --> 00:10:38.570
continue to preach that the
creative is the variable.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:39.700
So yes I do believe it.

00:10:39.700 --> 00:10:41.413
I believe if you watch a commercial

00:10:41.413 --> 00:10:45.673
and you think that product
sucks because of it, that's bad.

00:10:48.860 --> 00:10:49.793
- Okay.

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:53.520
So speaking of great creative,

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:56.620
I loved the Bubly Buble commercial

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:58.440
and got such a chuckle out of it

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:03.440
and Stacy here is one of the
creators behind the commercial

00:11:03.540 --> 00:11:05.237
from PepsiCo.

00:11:05.237 --> 00:11:07.310
So Stacy, it's interesting

00:11:07.310 --> 00:11:11.990
because more than half of the
presubmitted Super Bowl ads

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:13.740
had celebrities in them

00:11:13.740 --> 00:11:17.642
including your Bubly Buble commercial

00:11:17.642 --> 00:11:20.520
yet you used humor.

00:11:20.520 --> 00:11:22.080
Do you think celebrity

00:11:22.080 --> 00:11:24.810
is the greatest driver
of sentiment, humor?

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:27.030
Tell us what was behind
your thinking in that

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:30.180
and how those individual items,

00:11:30.180 --> 00:11:31.230
celebrity, humor,

00:11:31.230 --> 00:11:33.140
really make your decisions
and where you're going?

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:33.973
- Sure.

00:11:33.973 --> 00:11:34.806
First of all,

00:11:34.806 --> 00:11:38.860
also I think creative is the
utmost importance in this

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:40.160
and we know when we
look at mixed modeling,

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:41.631
just to build on that last point,

00:11:41.631 --> 00:11:44.990
two-thirds of all ROI and sales

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:46.230
comes from creative effectiveness

00:11:46.230 --> 00:11:47.270
more so than even the media.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:49.100
So it's hugely important

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:52.300
that we feel like we have
excellent creative at all times.

00:11:52.300 --> 00:11:54.300
And I think to answer your
question about the brand

00:11:54.300 --> 00:11:55.980
and about Bubly,

00:11:55.980 --> 00:11:58.290
the first thing is what
job are we trying to do

00:11:58.290 --> 00:11:59.680
with the Super Bowl ad.

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:01.530
And so a Pepsi and a Bubly

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:03.430
are doing two very different things.

00:12:03.430 --> 00:12:06.178
For Bubly, that brand has
been around less than a year,

00:12:06.178 --> 00:12:08.416
has relatively low awareness

00:12:08.416 --> 00:12:11.270
but is really catching
on and catching fire

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:12.850
and people are talking about it.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:14.300
And we said this is the moment,

00:12:14.300 --> 00:12:15.260
we're at an inflection point.

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:17.940
We want to drastically
increase our awareness.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:20.610
But do so in a way that's
consistent with the brand

00:12:20.610 --> 00:12:24.100
which is built on this idea of
fun and playful instigation.

00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:25.440
So when we were looking at creative,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.200
we didn't brief in has
to have a celebrity.

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:29.080
We didn't brief any of that.

00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:30.710
We briefed what our objectives were,

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:31.771
what the brand is about

00:12:31.771 --> 00:12:35.380
and then the creative that
best got the brand out

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:36.560
told people what it was

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.034
and did so in a way that
was worthy of Super Bowl

00:12:39.034 --> 00:12:41.590
was finding a celebrity whose name

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:45.280
happened to be very closely
related to the brand name.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:48.120
And so it was a little bit
of a happy coincidence.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:49.390
I think celebrity always helps

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:52.060
in driving PR impressions, talk value.

00:12:52.060 --> 00:12:53.270
But we would never have done it

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:55.313
at the expense of landing
the brand message.

00:12:55.313 --> 00:12:57.692
- So as a follow-up question,

00:12:57.692 --> 00:13:00.140
Bubly didn't have a lot of
advertising, like you said,

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:01.730
or awareness prior to the Super Bowl.

00:13:01.730 --> 00:13:03.750
You went all in big with the Super Bowl.

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:05.760
Where do you go from here?

00:13:05.760 --> 00:13:07.490
- We'll have a full year of support

00:13:07.490 --> 00:13:08.330
this year on Bubly.

00:13:08.330 --> 00:13:10.240
So we're using this as a launch platform.

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:13.840
We had a full campaign,
pre, during and post

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.640
around digital and social

00:13:16.640 --> 00:13:17.856
and a lot of different channels.

00:13:17.856 --> 00:13:19.483
In fact, during the Super Bowl,

00:13:19.483 --> 00:13:21.880
we had teams going out
in a bunch of cities

00:13:21.880 --> 00:13:24.070
and putting Sharpies on our product

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:25.570
so that consumers could find Buble

00:13:25.570 --> 00:13:27.330
instead of Bubly the next day.

00:13:27.330 --> 00:13:29.960
And so we had a lot of fun with it.

00:13:29.960 --> 00:13:31.101
And going forward,

00:13:31.101 --> 00:13:34.427
we're gonna continue to have a full year

00:13:34.427 --> 00:13:37.020
of marketing and
advertising on this brand.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:38.350
We don't want to have a boom splat

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:41.070
where people find out about
Bubly and then forget about it.

00:13:41.070 --> 00:13:43.040
This is the beginning of a long journey.

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:46.120
- And what kind of sentiment
were you seeing in the social?

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.010
- Extremely positive, extremely positive.

00:13:48.010 --> 00:13:49.770
It was 15 to one positive to negative

00:13:49.770 --> 00:13:51.850
and it was called out in a lot of places

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:54.710
as one of the winners from
positive sentiment perspective.

00:13:54.710 --> 00:13:56.000
- That's great.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:59.900
So Anda, according to Twitter,

00:13:59.900 --> 00:14:01.496
24 hours after the game

00:14:01.496 --> 00:14:04.360
the words boring and Super Bowl

00:14:05.362 --> 00:14:08.510
were tweeted over 70,000 times

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:10.831
and the words worst and Super Bowl

00:14:10.831 --> 00:14:13.920
were tweeted together over 50,000 times.

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:16.070
So do you think that the circumstances

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:17.836
of the Super Bowl itself

00:14:17.836 --> 00:14:19.850
affect the effectiveness

00:14:19.850 --> 00:14:20.823
of the ads in the Super Bowl?

00:14:20.823 --> 00:14:22.880
Does the research that you're looking at

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:25.220
show a difference there?

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:26.053
- Yeah, 100%.

00:14:26.053 --> 00:14:26.886
I think it influences

00:14:26.886 --> 00:14:28.550
the level of attention that people have

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:30.768
when the breaks actually come in.

00:14:30.768 --> 00:14:33.870
I think the level of emotional intensity

00:14:33.870 --> 00:14:35.920
is also just not there.

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:37.370
I think if you're in a mood

00:14:37.370 --> 00:14:39.330
where you're rooting for someone

00:14:39.330 --> 00:14:42.237
and you're very heated and
talking with your family about it

00:14:42.237 --> 00:14:43.830
and then you see an ad that's great,

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:46.130
it just hits home in a different way.

00:14:46.130 --> 00:14:49.570
And so yeah, I do think
that the fact that the game

00:14:49.570 --> 00:14:50.840
as someone was telling me on my team

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:53.715
that it was the lowest score
in history for the Super Bowl,

00:14:53.715 --> 00:14:56.310
yeah the fact that it
wasn't a very exciting game,

00:14:56.310 --> 00:15:00.380
I definitely think made
the overall advertising

00:15:00.380 --> 00:15:01.570
just not look as exciting.

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.810
But I also think that
the overall advertising

00:15:03.810 --> 00:15:06.160
just wasn't as bold as it usually is.

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:07.550
And so from a creative standpoint,

00:15:07.550 --> 00:15:09.020
actually one of the things
that I was wondering

00:15:09.020 --> 00:15:12.250
is to what extent has the last two years

00:15:12.250 --> 00:15:16.370
of all these massive revolutions
that deal with diversity,

00:15:16.370 --> 00:15:17.960
to what extent has it made brands

00:15:17.960 --> 00:15:20.231
just be fearful about what they can say?

00:15:20.231 --> 00:15:21.566
And essentially turn,

00:15:21.566 --> 00:15:22.550
I think it's a good thing

00:15:22.550 --> 00:15:25.100
that a lot of brands turned
to comedy and kept it light.

00:15:25.100 --> 00:15:27.290
Because at the end of the
day, it is a football game.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:29.740
But what does that mean
from a creative standpoint?

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:31.820
How can you cater to everyone?

00:15:31.820 --> 00:15:33.580
Does that mean that you're
basically not catering

00:15:33.580 --> 00:15:35.040
to anyone in particular?

00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:37.240
And what sort of campaigns
is that good for?

00:15:38.211 --> 00:15:41.480
- So what kind of special
considerations then?

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:43.410
I mean and you can all answer this.

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:44.600
I mean at NBC Universal,

00:15:44.600 --> 00:15:49.480
we had the Super Bowl
every three or four years

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:53.020
and we actually built an AI optimizer

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:55.082
to help advertisers look at the creative

00:15:55.082 --> 00:15:57.210
and help them improve it.

00:15:57.210 --> 00:15:58.670
So there are things,

00:15:58.670 --> 00:16:00.630
we look at the creative itself,

00:16:00.630 --> 00:16:03.410
do you use a celebrity, do
you use dogs or children?

00:16:03.410 --> 00:16:05.570
Those tend to sell really well.

00:16:05.570 --> 00:16:06.500
The messaging,

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:08.130
how long do you keep
your logo on the screen,

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:09.776
all these kinds of things.

00:16:09.776 --> 00:16:13.980
And help marketers drive impact.

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:17.700
What kind of considerations
do you tell marketers to use?

00:16:17.700 --> 00:16:18.840
You can speak,

00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:21.780
all of you I think should
answer this question.

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:25.250
- You know it's interesting
how you set up the question

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:27.290
with dogs and children.

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:29.520
Nothing gets more engagement on social

00:16:29.520 --> 00:16:30.805
than dogs and children

00:16:30.805 --> 00:16:34.160
and nothing sells less
product for businesses

00:16:34.160 --> 00:16:35.363
than dogs and children.

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:39.408
So I tell clients a lot when
we first start talking to them,

00:16:39.408 --> 00:16:41.240
when they want to make engagement

00:16:41.240 --> 00:16:43.654
or other metrics that have
nothing to do with their business

00:16:43.654 --> 00:16:46.110
as the KPI for our existence.

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:47.850
I literally look them
dead in the face and go,

00:16:47.850 --> 00:16:49.740
my ability to game that

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:51.590
and be the greatest you've ever seen

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:53.290
is so scary good

00:16:53.290 --> 00:16:55.710
but you probably won't
see the business results.

00:16:55.710 --> 00:16:57.617
- How do you know that dogs
and children don't sell?

00:16:57.617 --> 00:16:59.720
- Well because one of the great things

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:01.550
about how Vayner looks at things

00:17:01.550 --> 00:17:03.920
is because we have creative
and media under one roof

00:17:03.920 --> 00:17:07.070
and the only KPI we trade on is selling

00:17:07.070 --> 00:17:10.080
whether DTC on Amazon
or their own platform

00:17:10.080 --> 00:17:12.890
or doing massive holdout clusters

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:15.011
in Walmart's or things of that nature,

00:17:15.011 --> 00:17:18.380
what was unique about how
our business was built

00:17:18.380 --> 00:17:22.140
was we weren't one
discipline or the other.

00:17:22.140 --> 00:17:24.720
I didn't know anything about
the ad world when I started it.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:26.940
And so because I was
building it for myself

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:29.810
with the hope of buying
businesses and running through it,

00:17:29.810 --> 00:17:32.820
our religion from nine
years ago still to this day

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:34.620
has always been business results.

00:17:34.620 --> 00:17:36.670
And so when we look at,

00:17:36.670 --> 00:17:37.710
in the beginning of my career

00:17:37.710 --> 00:17:38.840
when I would look at reports,

00:17:38.840 --> 00:17:40.470
whether it was traditional reports

00:17:40.470 --> 00:17:42.050
or things like data logics that came along

00:17:42.050 --> 00:17:44.070
that helped Facebook justify,

00:17:44.070 --> 00:17:46.100
when I was looking at internal MMMs

00:17:46.100 --> 00:17:47.870
and all these things were doing one thing

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:50.270
but then the actual
business was doing another,

00:17:50.270 --> 00:17:52.180
and then the cliche answers of like

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:53.760
because media and creative were split,

00:17:53.760 --> 00:17:55.450
it was their fault or that fault,

00:17:55.450 --> 00:17:57.942
it became a very interesting model.

00:17:57.942 --> 00:17:59.930
As we've gotten deeper into the process,

00:17:59.930 --> 00:18:02.220
the beauty of Amazon
and direct-to-consumer

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:05.320
or the sophistication that happened

00:18:05.320 --> 00:18:07.610
as we built a big analytics
and media department

00:18:07.610 --> 00:18:12.610
that allowed us to hold out
regions, stores, locations

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:14.760
with consistency over
three years, sales lifts--

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:17.853
- And so when we look at
testing Super Bowl ads,

00:18:18.888 --> 00:18:20.150
we use Blink and Miller Brown

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:22.440
and some of the same tools
that we typically use

00:18:22.440 --> 00:18:23.820
and we look at all the same metrics

00:18:23.820 --> 00:18:25.910
and then we add viral potential

00:18:25.910 --> 00:18:27.400
just for Super Bowl ads.

00:18:27.400 --> 00:18:30.790
So still brand linkage and
engagement and enjoyment

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:32.720
are still the most important things

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:34.730
but then we also add this as extra metric

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:36.660
to make sure that it has talk value.

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:40.080
But we would never sacrifice
brand linkage or anything else

00:18:40.080 --> 00:18:42.376
in favor of that if that makes sense.

00:18:42.376 --> 00:18:45.020
- Anda, is there any research
or stats that you have

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:46.950
that marketers should consider

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:49.340
when they're making Super Bowl ads?

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:50.173
- Do you know, it's interesting.

00:18:50.173 --> 00:18:53.390
I think one of my friend's
companies, Neural Insight,

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:58.020
was researching I think
10 of the ads in advance

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.080
and kind of helping with
the creative pretesting

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:01.640
and as it turns out

00:19:01.640 --> 00:19:03.020
from both his work and ours,

00:19:03.020 --> 00:19:04.180
what we see time and time again

00:19:04.180 --> 00:19:05.310
is that what's right for one brand

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:06.540
is not right for another brand.

00:19:06.540 --> 00:19:09.284
And so I agree with what
you're saying about dogs,

00:19:09.284 --> 00:19:13.013
although I do remember that
the Budweiser puppy thing is,

00:19:13.850 --> 00:19:15.440
I have a good feeling about Budweiser

00:19:15.440 --> 00:19:17.180
and I've never bought it but--

00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:18.013
- That's an issue.

00:19:18.013 --> 00:19:19.340
- Yeah that is an issue.

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:20.992
I don't drink beer.
- That's the fucking

00:19:20.992 --> 00:19:22.337
punch line of all of this.

00:19:22.337 --> 00:19:26.260
- But if there was puppy
next to a bottle of red wine,

00:19:26.260 --> 00:19:28.660
I might have more favorability towards it.

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:31.330
But anyways, what works for a brand

00:19:31.330 --> 00:19:32.500
doesn't work for another brand.

00:19:32.500 --> 00:19:34.560
And so I think it's not
about trying to find,

00:19:34.560 --> 00:19:36.240
I think our industry,
in general, is obsessed

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:38.710
with benchmarks and trying
to find these golden nuggets

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:40.460
that are going to transform your brand.

00:19:40.460 --> 00:19:41.940
And that's not the case.

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:43.400
I think maybe if anything,

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:45.720
from both a personal standpoint

00:19:45.720 --> 00:19:49.010
and what we've seen from a
brand awareness perspective

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:50.930
is that the constant mention of a name,

00:19:50.930 --> 00:19:52.560
especially when that name is not known

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:53.873
is really, really important.

00:19:53.873 --> 00:19:55.920
Actually one of my
favorite ads from last year

00:19:55.920 --> 00:19:56.913
was the Tide ad.

00:19:56.913 --> 00:19:57.809
I don't know if you remember it

00:19:57.809 --> 00:20:00.770
but basically it was
just the funniest thing.

00:20:00.770 --> 00:20:02.133
You kept thinking it was this other ad

00:20:02.133 --> 00:20:03.130
but then it was a Tide ad

00:20:03.130 --> 00:20:04.630
and they kept repeating that to you

00:20:04.630 --> 00:20:05.670
and I came out of that

00:20:05.670 --> 00:20:08.830
and again, I guess I haven't
bought Tide but at least--

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:09.943
- That also matters.

00:20:12.763 --> 00:20:15.470
And I think what really
is exciting for me,

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:19.320
and look, I'm a buyer of Super Bowl.

00:20:19.320 --> 00:20:20.530
When NBC gets it again,

00:20:20.530 --> 00:20:22.340
these were five million, make it 10

00:20:22.340 --> 00:20:23.900
because I think they're really underpriced

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:25.364
and I mean that.
- Please don't.

00:20:25.364 --> 00:20:26.364
- I mean it.

00:20:27.853 --> 00:20:30.920
I think they're grossly
underpriced for the attention,

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:31.753
I really do.

00:20:31.753 --> 00:20:33.250
When you look at the
delta between Super Bowl

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:35.550
and then the next 10 most expensive ads,

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:36.994
whether it's the AFC/NFC
championship game,

00:20:36.994 --> 00:20:40.019
the NBA finals, the Grammy's, the Oscar's.

00:20:40.019 --> 00:20:41.206
They're expensive

00:20:41.206 --> 00:20:44.280
and yet nothing like this is gonna happen.

00:20:44.280 --> 00:20:46.032
So I think they're very underpriced.

00:20:46.032 --> 00:20:47.653
But I think the most important thing

00:20:47.653 --> 00:20:50.810
that happened so far in this talk for me

00:20:50.810 --> 00:20:52.930
is when you said I didn't buy it.

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:55.960
But maybe if that dog was
next to a glass of wine

00:20:55.960 --> 00:20:56.969
it'd be more interesting.

00:20:56.969 --> 00:20:58.898
There's something called Facebook

00:20:58.898 --> 00:21:01.400
and Google and YouTube and Twitter

00:21:01.400 --> 00:21:04.430
which actually has remarkable data

00:21:04.430 --> 00:21:07.860
to understand that you
do have a propensity

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:10.610
to like red wine or dogs
or things of that nature

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:13.390
and we can put those ads in front of you.

00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:15.460
I'm super fascinated

00:21:15.460 --> 00:21:17.860
by the unbelievable capabilities

00:21:17.860 --> 00:21:19.411
of some of the modern platforms

00:21:19.411 --> 00:21:21.490
to do the thing that you just said

00:21:21.490 --> 00:21:22.940
that is the most true thing

00:21:22.940 --> 00:21:24.590
which is it has to resonate,

00:21:24.590 --> 00:21:25.800
back to your point earlier.

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:28.353
What's really tough
about television for me,

00:21:28.353 --> 00:21:30.760
I'm obsessed with the overall awareness

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:32.560
back to the names and
things of that nature.

00:21:32.560 --> 00:21:35.220
My favorite ad in the last
10 years I've stumbled upon

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:36.230
was a Versace ad

00:21:36.230 --> 00:21:38.730
where the entire ad is just
people saying the word Versace.

00:21:38.730 --> 00:21:41.650
Like I'm a buyer of
certain things like that.

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:45.120
But after this and what it does,

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:46.870
I'm so obsessed with the fact

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:48.940
that we actually now do live in a world

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:52.120
where puppy, red wine gets
in front of you does exist

00:21:52.120 --> 00:21:55.253
and yet there's a
disproportionate underestimation

00:21:55.253 --> 00:21:57.840
of what that actually means.

00:21:57.840 --> 00:21:58.910
- But I think what you're saying

00:21:58.910 --> 00:22:00.140
also taps into the fact

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:03.210
that there's segmentation when it comes

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:05.140
to sending the right
message to the right person

00:22:05.140 --> 00:22:07.240
and then maybe in the
context of the Super Bowl,

00:22:07.240 --> 00:22:08.370
given the fact that you're reaching

00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:09.430
so many people at the same time,

00:22:09.430 --> 00:22:11.260
what you're really trying to do

00:22:11.260 --> 00:22:12.827
is either launch a new product

00:22:12.827 --> 00:22:15.520
or try to change your brand's perception.

00:22:15.520 --> 00:22:17.940
But for anything else
that's more targeted,

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:20.540
for stuff like Tide that
everyone already knows about,

00:22:20.540 --> 00:22:21.920
is it as effective?

00:22:21.920 --> 00:22:23.710
Would you pay 10 million for that?

00:22:23.710 --> 00:22:24.543
- Somebody said to me,

00:22:24.543 --> 00:22:28.290
Gary but we have 99%
brand awareness already.

00:22:28.290 --> 00:22:30.831
And I go and eight percent relevance.

00:22:30.831 --> 00:22:32.740
The reality is consideration.

00:22:32.740 --> 00:22:33.573
To your point,

00:22:33.573 --> 00:22:35.233
and I think you're making the right point,

00:22:36.360 --> 00:22:38.550
we use the word content so much

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:41.360
and we don't use the word context enough.

00:22:41.360 --> 00:22:43.060
And I think a lot of where you're going

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:43.970
is exactly right.

00:22:43.970 --> 00:22:45.043
And so for me,

00:22:46.380 --> 00:22:49.600
I'm just fascinated that one,

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:51.100
and it's funny you brought
up the Denny's thing

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:53.223
'cause that actually happened
like seven or eight years ago.

00:22:53.223 --> 00:22:54.550
- Yeah it was awhile ago

00:22:54.550 --> 00:22:55.550
and they haven't done it since

00:22:55.550 --> 00:22:56.520
and they won't again.

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:58.460
- Because their product sucks.

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:01.200
If you think about what they
weren't able to deliver on,

00:23:01.200 --> 00:23:03.090
they actually got people in.

00:23:03.090 --> 00:23:04.922
So I've been watching
it for a long time too

00:23:04.922 --> 00:23:06.832
and I want to hear what--

00:23:06.832 --> 00:23:07.943
- I think that's relative
if it sucks or not,

00:23:07.943 --> 00:23:09.350
depends on the audience.

00:23:09.350 --> 00:23:11.740
I think a lot of products that
were on the Super Bowl suck.

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:13.280
- No, no, the product sucked

00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:16.160
because they were able to get
a massive influx of users,

00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:16.993
CAC,

00:23:16.993 --> 00:23:19.410
but they weren't able to
retain it, LTV, right?

00:23:19.410 --> 00:23:21.480
- Yeah, so they basically
figured that out and said,

00:23:21.480 --> 00:23:23.610
we can basically go back for scale

00:23:23.610 --> 00:23:25.075
until we figure this out internally.

00:23:25.075 --> 00:23:26.180
- Correct.

00:23:26.180 --> 00:23:27.760
But what they did worked.

00:23:27.760 --> 00:23:30.020
To me, what I loved about that ad

00:23:30.020 --> 00:23:32.140
and have for the last seven to eight years

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:33.280
is it worked.

00:23:33.280 --> 00:23:35.610
It was more utilitarian

00:23:35.610 --> 00:23:36.460
which I loved about it

00:23:36.460 --> 00:23:38.900
and which I think is a need
within this Super Bowl.

00:23:38.900 --> 00:23:40.850
Their unique issue, to your point

00:23:40.850 --> 00:23:42.810
was the product didn't allow them

00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:45.403
to get the value in their LTV

00:23:45.403 --> 00:23:47.710
because of the product.

00:23:47.710 --> 00:23:50.290
A lot of things that happen in
advertising are funny to me.

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:51.520
We win and we lose

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:53.430
predicated on the actual thing.

00:23:53.430 --> 00:23:55.080
There's a lot of people
that are part of successes

00:23:55.080 --> 00:23:56.840
'cause the product was phenomenal

00:23:56.840 --> 00:23:58.400
and the advertising was just fine.

00:23:58.400 --> 00:23:59.590
The product was phenomenal.

00:23:59.590 --> 00:24:01.727
So there's a lot to unpack there

00:24:01.727 --> 00:24:03.333
but I do think context is something

00:24:03.333 --> 00:24:05.080
that we need to talk more about

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:07.320
and utility to the Super Bowl

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:09.927
I think is an untapped opportunity.

00:24:09.927 --> 00:24:11.490
- Let's talk about it.

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:14.290
So can you change people's opinions?

00:24:14.290 --> 00:24:19.260
The NFL, they had their ad
for the 100 years of the NFL.

00:24:19.260 --> 00:24:20.350
I'm not sure if you saw that one.

00:24:20.350 --> 00:24:22.080
It had so many players.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:24.290
It was ranked number one by USA Today

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:28.513
which is actually regular people
ranking the Super Bowl ads.

00:24:28.513 --> 00:24:31.330
Do you think that they,

00:24:31.330 --> 00:24:33.630
with all the controversy that they've had

00:24:33.630 --> 00:24:36.610
that that ad has now changed the sentiment

00:24:36.610 --> 00:24:38.483
of how people feel about the NFL?

00:24:39.820 --> 00:24:41.420
- I think it helped.

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:44.440
And I think they have a
long way to climb back from

00:24:44.440 --> 00:24:47.210
but what I liked about that ad

00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:49.740
besides the fact that I feel
like it was a little cheating

00:24:49.740 --> 00:24:50.573
'cause they didn't have to pay

00:24:50.573 --> 00:24:53.038
for the hotel and the
air time and everything,

00:24:53.038 --> 00:24:53.871
and it was two minutes long,

00:24:53.871 --> 00:24:55.350
but it was just fun.

00:24:55.350 --> 00:24:57.100
It was what people like about football.

00:24:57.100 --> 00:25:00.770
It was fumbles and
interceptions and throwing balls

00:25:00.770 --> 00:25:04.170
and people falling down and
all the talent that we all love

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:07.200
and that's what everyone's
craving from the NFL,

00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:10.270
not all the other baggage that
has come with it this year.

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:12.230
And so I applaud them

00:25:12.230 --> 00:25:13.280
'cause I think they got back

00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:15.860
to what people want to see from them.

00:25:15.860 --> 00:25:18.040
- It definitely didn't
change anybody's mind

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:20.150
whose disproportionately overly passionate

00:25:20.150 --> 00:25:22.260
about the concussion issues the league has

00:25:22.260 --> 00:25:24.120
or the flag issues.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:25.501
No different than Gillette.

00:25:25.501 --> 00:25:27.734
One of the things that's
so important about ads

00:25:27.734 --> 00:25:30.640
when things are going on in real life

00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:33.241
is understanding where
people sit on issues.

00:25:33.241 --> 00:25:35.940
So like if you're a mother of a son

00:25:35.940 --> 00:25:38.860
whose got five concussions
deep in high school

00:25:38.860 --> 00:25:40.280
and is starting to deal with issues

00:25:40.280 --> 00:25:41.630
because of the sport of football

00:25:41.630 --> 00:25:43.030
that you're blaming for that,

00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:44.370
that ad's doing nothing for you.

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:45.970
If you're somebody
completely in the middle

00:25:45.970 --> 00:25:47.700
and kind of like whatever,

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:49.270
maybe it did move you a little bit.

00:25:49.270 --> 00:25:53.070
And so I think that's
an important variable

00:25:53.070 --> 00:25:54.090
in those kind of things.

00:25:54.090 --> 00:25:57.180
- I think it's so hard to change
perception with one thing,

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.820
especially one thing that airs
during your biggest event.

00:25:59.820 --> 00:26:01.653
I think you have to try harder.

00:26:02.760 --> 00:26:06.002
And do it at scale and use
volume as well as context

00:26:06.002 --> 00:26:08.380
throughout the entire
year to get that done.

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:09.213
- The other thing is

00:26:09.213 --> 00:26:10.586
I think we talk a lot about things

00:26:10.586 --> 00:26:13.020
and everybody here is a keyboard warrior

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:16.240
and a real champion out there
publicly on social media

00:26:16.240 --> 00:26:17.800
but then our actions are different.

00:26:17.800 --> 00:26:19.460
So there's a lot of people

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:20.293
that say a lot of things

00:26:20.293 --> 00:26:21.750
and then act differently.

00:26:21.750 --> 00:26:24.670
NFL ratings did quite nicely this year

00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:27.370
because the games played out
differently on primetime.

00:26:28.479 --> 00:26:30.060
I think there's a lot of,

00:26:30.060 --> 00:26:34.620
the level of hypocrisy in
our society is staggering

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:36.950
and people are tough
guys and gals on Twitter

00:26:36.950 --> 00:26:38.180
about products and services

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:40.730
and then they spend their
money in a different way.

00:26:41.634 --> 00:26:44.960
- So speaking of products and services,

00:26:44.960 --> 00:26:47.360
Verizon, they took a route

00:26:47.360 --> 00:26:49.820
of acknowledging first responders

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:52.193
and then tying it back to the NFL.

00:26:52.193 --> 00:26:56.210
Do you think that that made
a strong impact for brand

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:59.540
or did it get, were people confused?

00:26:59.540 --> 00:27:03.233
How does this have to do
with my wireless service?

00:27:03.233 --> 00:27:05.300
Stacy go ahead.
- I'll let you go first.

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:07.480
- Yeah I mean I think some of those,

00:27:07.480 --> 00:27:10.670
I think Verizon and T-Mobile and auto,

00:27:10.670 --> 00:27:12.120
I think a lot of those industries

00:27:12.120 --> 00:27:15.850
are really hard to land
the what brand it's for.

00:27:15.850 --> 00:27:18.120
I thought Verizon did a really good job

00:27:18.120 --> 00:27:20.990
and I thought linking
it to the NFL players

00:27:20.990 --> 00:27:23.310
and being contextually
relevant made a lot of sense.

00:27:23.310 --> 00:27:24.425
I'm not sure anyone,

00:27:24.425 --> 00:27:26.850
in a few months people are gonna remember

00:27:26.850 --> 00:27:27.930
that it was Verizon.

00:27:27.930 --> 00:27:29.280
I think they'll remember the ad

00:27:29.280 --> 00:27:32.030
'cause it was a really
beautiful, powerful ad

00:27:32.030 --> 00:27:33.990
but I think it's really,

00:27:33.990 --> 00:27:35.150
to Gary's point,

00:27:35.150 --> 00:27:37.270
unless you have cell
phones all over these ads,

00:27:37.270 --> 00:27:39.930
I'm not sure it's landing
a product message.

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:41.010
If you remember it's Verizon,

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:42.460
you might think more positively about it

00:27:42.460 --> 00:27:44.148
but first you have to
remember it's Verizon.

00:27:44.148 --> 00:27:45.013
I don't know.

00:27:46.204 --> 00:27:49.477
- So we work with almost
all the big telcos.

00:27:49.477 --> 00:27:53.038
I think Verizon's the only
one we don't work with yet.

00:27:53.038 --> 00:27:55.640
So I can't speak to the data itself

00:27:55.640 --> 00:27:56.473
but what I will say

00:27:56.473 --> 00:27:57.970
is that by and large,

00:27:57.970 --> 00:27:59.470
almost all of the other big telcos

00:27:59.470 --> 00:28:02.090
aren't making necessarily
this type of advertising.

00:28:02.090 --> 00:28:05.420
They're a lot more focused on
the innovation, utility piece

00:28:05.420 --> 00:28:06.943
and the educational piece.

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:09.934
For what it's worth as a consumer,

00:28:09.934 --> 00:28:14.934
I've been exposed to a bunch
of their videos in the series

00:28:15.250 --> 00:28:18.240
and I've actually remembered
that Verizon is the company

00:28:18.240 --> 00:28:20.671
that helps first responders
pick up the call.

00:28:20.671 --> 00:28:22.750
And for what it's worth

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:25.430
it's made my perception more positive.

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:26.720
I think there's a long way to go now

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:29.050
in terms of educating me
why it's a better option

00:28:29.050 --> 00:28:32.650
but from a perception
standpoint, it's worked for me

00:28:32.650 --> 00:28:33.483
and I love the ad.

00:28:33.483 --> 00:28:35.259
And the thing I love the most about it

00:28:35.259 --> 00:28:39.120
is that it actually created
a thread for me over time

00:28:39.120 --> 00:28:40.570
as opposed to just this one thing.

00:28:40.570 --> 00:28:42.070
I think if it had been just one thing,

00:28:42.070 --> 00:28:43.130
I agree with you,

00:28:43.130 --> 00:28:45.180
I probably would have forgotten about it.

00:28:46.048 --> 00:28:47.994
- Want to say anything about it?

00:28:47.994 --> 00:28:49.733
- I didn't love it.

00:28:50.651 --> 00:28:52.060
- It didn't hit your heart strings?

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:52.893
- Nope.

00:28:52.893 --> 00:28:54.269
- Why didn't you love it?

00:28:54.269 --> 00:28:56.055
- I didn't remember it.

00:28:56.055 --> 00:28:57.190
I watched it again,

00:28:57.190 --> 00:28:58.513
it came at the end.

00:28:59.400 --> 00:29:01.390
I just thought it was poorly executed.

00:29:01.390 --> 00:29:03.250
I hate things that happen at the end,

00:29:03.250 --> 00:29:06.148
like the URLs that people
are trying to send it to you.

00:29:06.148 --> 00:29:07.820
ESPN established this 15 years ago,

00:29:07.820 --> 00:29:09.590
put it at the bottom the whole time.

00:29:09.590 --> 00:29:11.520
You're asking people,

00:29:11.520 --> 00:29:12.610
I don't know, I just didn't love it

00:29:12.610 --> 00:29:13.550
and I also think look,

00:29:13.550 --> 00:29:17.060
I'm always thinking about
things like this of like

00:29:18.290 --> 00:29:22.060
what normal human being is
going through the process

00:29:22.060 --> 00:29:23.300
that we are right now?

00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:24.300
None.

00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:26.140
And so I'm trying to understand,

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:28.130
do we become caricatures of ourselves

00:29:28.130 --> 00:29:29.490
'cause we're in the bubble

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:31.990
and are analyzing it at a
level that is just not real

00:29:31.990 --> 00:29:33.430
or is this us synthesizing

00:29:33.430 --> 00:29:35.310
what people are actually going through?

00:29:35.310 --> 00:29:36.210
But for me I didn't love it

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:37.870
because I didn't remember it.

00:29:37.870 --> 00:29:40.225
I didn't remember it when it showed here.

00:29:40.225 --> 00:29:42.050
And even the way it was executed.

00:29:42.050 --> 00:29:44.740
I don't love the unveil
at the end kind of thing

00:29:44.740 --> 00:29:47.160
and it didn't pull at my heart strings,

00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:51.480
not that I'm thrilled somebody's
got to be the phone service

00:29:51.480 --> 00:29:52.770
for first responders.

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:53.603
I don't know, not for me.

00:29:53.603 --> 00:29:55.440
- I think you hit on a
really important point

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:56.430
which is simplicity.

00:29:56.430 --> 00:30:00.120
So I think when you have a
lot of exposure to a campaign

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.970
and you study it and you
watch it a few times,

00:30:02.970 --> 00:30:03.803
it's different.

00:30:03.803 --> 00:30:05.010
The vast majority

00:30:05.010 --> 00:30:06.775
of the 100 million people
that are gonna see this

00:30:06.775 --> 00:30:08.870
have two seconds,

00:30:08.870 --> 00:30:10.284
they look at it, they might chuckle,

00:30:10.284 --> 00:30:12.020
they might feel something in their heart

00:30:12.020 --> 00:30:13.280
and then they're on to the next thing.

00:30:13.280 --> 00:30:15.148
And so keeping it simple, I think,

00:30:15.148 --> 00:30:16.657
can be really important.

00:30:16.657 --> 00:30:18.400
- Yeah and the vast majority

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:20.560
is actually 99.99%,

00:30:20.560 --> 00:30:21.920
right?
- Yeah, exactly, right.

00:30:21.920 --> 00:30:23.590
- But speaking of pulling
at the heart strings,

00:30:23.590 --> 00:30:24.700
it wasn't one of the ads we showed

00:30:24.700 --> 00:30:28.394
but the Microsoft really
pulled at the heart strings

00:30:28.394 --> 00:30:32.360
yet I'm not so sure that I was left

00:30:32.360 --> 00:30:33.740
knowing what the product was.

00:30:33.740 --> 00:30:36.710
I knew it had something
to do with video games.

00:30:36.710 --> 00:30:39.380
Anyone want to comment on that?

00:30:39.380 --> 00:30:41.570
- I just think high consideration products

00:30:41.570 --> 00:30:45.190
are never gonna do that
well at the Super Bowl.

00:30:45.190 --> 00:30:46.700
I think low consideration products

00:30:46.700 --> 00:30:48.040
that need to get differentiated,

00:30:48.040 --> 00:30:49.300
that need to get launched,

00:30:49.300 --> 00:30:50.330
that need brand awareness,

00:30:50.330 --> 00:30:51.680
are gonna be good

00:30:51.680 --> 00:30:53.410
but any high consideration product

00:30:53.410 --> 00:30:55.720
that has a longer sale cycle, very tough.

00:30:55.720 --> 00:30:57.030
- So can I ask you a question on that?

00:30:57.030 --> 00:30:57.863
- Yeah.

00:30:57.863 --> 00:31:01.150
- So those kind of items on regular TVC,

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:03.119
outside of Super Bowl?

00:31:03.119 --> 00:31:05.080
- No, I think, again, if you're trying

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:07.770
to talk about the awareness

00:31:07.770 --> 00:31:10.010
or get the awareness, it might work.

00:31:10.010 --> 00:31:12.780
I think from an educational standpoint,

00:31:12.780 --> 00:31:14.290
30 seconds doesn't help with that.

00:31:14.290 --> 00:31:15.880
I think you need a lot of content

00:31:15.880 --> 00:31:18.030
and I think you need to
target it at the right people

00:31:18.030 --> 00:31:18.880
at the right time.

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:20.363
- Yeah, that's why the awareness,

00:31:20.363 --> 00:31:22.170
that's the thing I'm
trying to work through

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:23.003
which I appreciate.

00:31:23.003 --> 00:31:24.070
I think that's exactly right.

00:31:24.070 --> 00:31:26.883
Awareness, and then I'm
trying to understand the delta

00:31:26.883 --> 00:31:30.222
of literally every other
commercial but the Super Bowl's

00:31:30.222 --> 00:31:32.320
ability to actually get awareness

00:31:32.320 --> 00:31:33.630
and actually be consumed,

00:31:33.630 --> 00:31:35.890
not potential GRPs.

00:31:35.890 --> 00:31:39.329
And so that's an interesting
concept of awareness

00:31:39.329 --> 00:31:41.510
in a medium that,

00:31:41.510 --> 00:31:43.960
it's scary for me to
think what this audience

00:31:43.960 --> 00:31:46.300
let alone or anybody in America,

00:31:46.300 --> 00:31:51.210
their behavior switches to
Amazon Prime, Hulu and Netflix

00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:52.794
as just where you consume period

00:31:52.794 --> 00:31:54.617
and then what's left

00:31:54.617 --> 00:31:57.440
over the last five years of commercials,

00:31:57.440 --> 00:31:59.090
what's actually being consumed

00:31:59.090 --> 00:32:01.070
versus looking at your phone.

00:32:01.070 --> 00:32:03.190
It starts getting to
scary territory for me

00:32:03.190 --> 00:32:05.160
for the ability to build awareness.

00:32:05.160 --> 00:32:06.891
- But look at GoDaddy.

00:32:06.891 --> 00:32:08.786
I just have to say GoDaddy,

00:32:08.786 --> 00:32:11.220
who knew about them before the Super Bowl?

00:32:11.220 --> 00:32:14.053
Was there any GoDaddy in
this year's Super Bowl?

00:32:15.390 --> 00:32:17.390
- Expensify tried

00:32:17.390 --> 00:32:19.810
and I was pumped that Two fucking
Chainz was in the building

00:32:19.810 --> 00:32:22.760
but I'm not sure if they pulled it off.

00:32:22.760 --> 00:32:24.480
- I would just go back to
Microsoft for a second though

00:32:24.480 --> 00:32:27.530
'cause I wonder if you're making that ad,

00:32:27.530 --> 00:32:29.160
if your objective is to really get people

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:31.320
to understand what that product does

00:32:31.320 --> 00:32:35.940
or if it was a brand perception
equity kind of driving thing

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:37.690
that is then supported by other media

00:32:37.690 --> 00:32:39.786
that sells the actual product.

00:32:39.786 --> 00:32:43.133
So it made a lot of people feel something

00:32:43.133 --> 00:32:46.860
and Microsoft doesn't often
make people feel something

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:47.693
the way Apple does.

00:32:47.693 --> 00:32:50.380
So I think that my guess
is it was more about

00:32:50.380 --> 00:32:51.920
trying to change their equity

00:32:51.920 --> 00:32:53.230
and change their perception a little bit,

00:32:53.230 --> 00:32:55.191
not so much about trying
to explain the product.

00:32:55.191 --> 00:32:56.410
- I don't know if this is just me

00:32:56.410 --> 00:32:58.840
but I think if you're trying to do that

00:32:58.840 --> 00:33:03.840
and trying to kind of aim
at your millennial hipster,

00:33:04.000 --> 00:33:07.470
I don't know if the Super
Bowl's the best time to do that.

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:08.430
I almost feel like

00:33:08.430 --> 00:33:09.500
if you are at the Super Bowl,

00:33:09.500 --> 00:33:11.910
you're not gonna be perceived
as this super cool brand

00:33:11.910 --> 00:33:14.290
that young people are going to embrace.

00:33:14.290 --> 00:33:16.670
So if you're trying to sell a
product towards that audience,

00:33:16.670 --> 00:33:17.648
I think it's tough.

00:33:17.648 --> 00:33:21.134
And again, like Microsoft
has great brand awareness

00:33:21.134 --> 00:33:23.350
I think when it comes to educating people

00:33:23.350 --> 00:33:25.680
and getting them to perceive
you as a cool brand,

00:33:25.680 --> 00:33:27.040
changing that perception,

00:33:27.040 --> 00:33:28.520
I think you can turn to other places

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:30.660
for less money and more target.

00:33:30.660 --> 00:33:31.573
That's just me.

00:33:33.720 --> 00:33:35.720
- I wasn't sure if I was
getting the five minute sign

00:33:35.720 --> 00:33:37.181
from the back?

00:33:37.181 --> 00:33:38.950
She's not paying attention.

00:33:38.950 --> 00:33:41.167
- Laura, do we have
more than five minutes?

00:33:41.167 --> 00:33:42.730
- Are you giving the five minute sign?

00:33:42.730 --> 00:33:44.303
I saw you hold up that--

00:33:44.303 --> 00:33:45.136
(speaking in distance)

00:33:45.136 --> 00:33:46.830
Okay, alright.

00:33:46.830 --> 00:33:47.663
- Just waving.

00:33:50.079 --> 00:33:50.912
- There's Q&amp;A.

00:33:50.912 --> 00:33:54.061
- Q&amp;A will be coming soon.

00:33:54.061 --> 00:33:56.340
So in your opinion,

00:33:56.340 --> 00:33:59.770
if a brand is gonna choose
one thing to shell out

00:33:59.770 --> 00:34:04.770
is it celebrity, licensing
an iconic piece of music

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:06.780
like Funky Town, CGI?

00:34:10.815 --> 00:34:12.370
God what else is there

00:34:12.370 --> 00:34:14.360
they could spend a lot of money on?

00:34:14.360 --> 00:34:16.650
Two minutes long commercial.

00:34:16.650 --> 00:34:18.010
What would you choose?

00:34:18.010 --> 00:34:20.730
Stacy, what would be the
thing that you would invest?

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:21.763
- It depends.

00:34:22.910 --> 00:34:23.743
No it does.

00:34:23.743 --> 00:34:24.576
I mean again,

00:34:24.576 --> 00:34:28.160
I think based on the
creative development process

00:34:28.160 --> 00:34:29.710
and what your objectives are

00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:31.100
and what your brand stands for,

00:34:31.100 --> 00:34:33.430
a brand like Bubly is
always gonna lean on humor

00:34:33.430 --> 00:34:35.900
and will more likely use a
piece of music or a celebrity

00:34:35.900 --> 00:34:36.930
or might use animation.

00:34:36.930 --> 00:34:39.880
But it depends on the creative idea.

00:34:39.880 --> 00:34:42.273
I think you do need
something for Super Bowl

00:34:42.273 --> 00:34:44.220
but I think that something needs to be

00:34:44.220 --> 00:34:45.970
an excellent creative idea

00:34:45.970 --> 00:34:48.046
and then the bells and
whistles that are around it

00:34:48.046 --> 00:34:49.310
should add to that,

00:34:49.310 --> 00:34:51.610
shouldn't cover up something that's just

00:34:51.610 --> 00:34:53.410
not good to begin with.

00:34:53.410 --> 00:34:55.740
So I wouldn't, I don't know,

00:34:55.740 --> 00:34:56.950
next year it could be something different

00:34:56.950 --> 00:34:58.450
than I would choose this year.

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:01.490
- Anda?

00:35:01.490 --> 00:35:03.300
- Look, if there's a formula for success,

00:35:03.300 --> 00:35:05.730
I think marketers would be billionaires

00:35:05.730 --> 00:35:09.050
and we would be out of
business as a data company.

00:35:09.050 --> 00:35:11.810
I think there's different things

00:35:11.810 --> 00:35:13.130
that have worked for different brands.

00:35:13.130 --> 00:35:13.963
At a high level,

00:35:13.963 --> 00:35:16.910
the things that I've been
saying throughout the panel

00:35:16.910 --> 00:35:19.040
is I would use it for brand awareness

00:35:19.040 --> 00:35:21.430
and I would try to mention
the name a million times

00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:23.880
to make sure that people
do stick with something,

00:35:23.880 --> 00:35:25.453
even if it's a 30 second slot.

00:35:27.143 --> 00:35:28.330
Take someone like Bumble.

00:35:28.330 --> 00:35:30.270
I think if I were an innovative company,

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:33.550
I wouldn't necessarily turn
to the Super Bowl for this.

00:35:33.550 --> 00:35:36.500
I think there's potentially
a negative brand equity

00:35:36.500 --> 00:35:38.490
that you get amongst young people

00:35:38.490 --> 00:35:41.810
if you turn to the Super
Bowl for awareness.

00:35:41.810 --> 00:35:43.013
So that's just me.

00:35:44.094 --> 00:35:45.423
- Two minutes.

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:48.380
- You would spend the
money on the two minutes.

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:50.367
- In the way you asked the question, yes.

00:35:50.367 --> 00:35:51.200
- Okay.

00:35:52.350 --> 00:35:54.375
- I just think it's grossly
underpriced attention

00:35:54.375 --> 00:35:57.129
and then to everything
you're hearing up here

00:35:57.129 --> 00:35:59.880
the variable is the creative

00:35:59.880 --> 00:36:02.563
and the moment in time and the context.

00:36:04.053 --> 00:36:07.850
I think that the price
is very, very underpriced

00:36:07.850 --> 00:36:10.270
mainly because of the
reality of businesses.

00:36:10.270 --> 00:36:12.940
I'm stunned by how much the
biggest brands in the world

00:36:12.940 --> 00:36:16.040
are willing to spend on television
outside of the Super Bowl

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:19.220
and I'd prefer them just
buy Super Bowl at scale

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:21.620
and then take their dollars elsewhere

00:36:21.620 --> 00:36:23.920
in some of the contextual other channels

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:24.930
that we've referenced.

00:36:24.930 --> 00:36:25.800
So I think it's important

00:36:25.800 --> 00:36:27.500
to not take it out of a silo

00:36:27.500 --> 00:36:29.397
of what's actually happening out there.

00:36:29.397 --> 00:36:31.890
And so what's actually happening out there

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:33.300
is there's brands
sitting there and saying,

00:36:33.300 --> 00:36:34.800
ooh, Super Bowl's expensive.

00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:38.860
Let me just spend 3.7 or 5.7
through the year on television

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:41.100
and I'm just deeply in belief

00:36:41.100 --> 00:36:42.010
that they are just getting

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:44.020
absolutely no consumption of that.

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:45.780
And they're getting no consumption

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:46.940
in many digital places too,

00:36:46.940 --> 00:36:48.436
programmatic banners and many other.

00:36:48.436 --> 00:36:50.720
There's a stunning amount of money

00:36:50.720 --> 00:36:52.414
being thrown directly in the trash

00:36:52.414 --> 00:36:55.362
and I think Super Bowl is
not one of those places.

00:36:55.362 --> 00:36:59.120
And I think current capability
of the social platforms

00:36:59.120 --> 00:37:00.400
are not one of those places.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:01.800
And then finding the creative

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:03.410
that's contextual for those platforms

00:37:03.410 --> 00:37:05.570
is very, very important during this time

00:37:05.570 --> 00:37:07.220
of the haves and have nots

00:37:07.220 --> 00:37:09.690
which is why we're seeing
so many brands decline.

00:37:09.690 --> 00:37:12.350
Because when you're wasting
95 cents on the dollar,

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:14.600
you're gonna get in trouble.

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:15.433
- Quick question,

00:37:15.433 --> 00:37:18.030
do you actually believe that
every brand could benefit

00:37:18.030 --> 00:37:18.870
from the Super Bowl?

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:21.378
Like lets say Salesforce or Knotch

00:37:21.378 --> 00:37:24.160
could benefit from the Super Bowl?

00:37:24.160 --> 00:37:25.110
- It's an interesting question.

00:37:25.110 --> 00:37:27.710
I agree with where your macro statement is

00:37:27.710 --> 00:37:28.922
that there's a time and place

00:37:28.922 --> 00:37:31.140
but I will say this.

00:37:31.140 --> 00:37:33.580
I do believe that every
brand can benefit from,

00:37:33.580 --> 00:37:35.500
so the answer to your question

00:37:35.500 --> 00:37:37.100
directly the way you asked it,

00:37:37.100 --> 00:37:39.300
no, I don't think every brand can.

00:37:39.300 --> 00:37:42.160
However, that gets in
contradiction with my belief

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:44.410
that it is so grossly underpriced

00:37:44.410 --> 00:37:47.741
that if they were to understand
what to do with that time,

00:37:47.741 --> 00:37:49.220
I think what we're trying to do

00:37:49.220 --> 00:37:51.140
is too much entertainment and branding.

00:37:51.140 --> 00:37:52.310
Back to utility,

00:37:52.310 --> 00:37:53.730
I think that Salesforce,

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:55.400
if they ran their ad and said,

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:57.280
go to Salesforce.com right now

00:37:57.280 --> 00:38:00.430
and fill this out if you're in B2B at all

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:01.770
or we're introducing this thing.

00:38:01.770 --> 00:38:03.522
I do think the awareness

00:38:03.522 --> 00:38:06.070
which I think we all understand has value

00:38:06.070 --> 00:38:07.690
is actually there.

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:08.523
To me it's like,

00:38:08.523 --> 00:38:10.620
it's crazy that it's actually there.

00:38:10.620 --> 00:38:12.184
I don't know if I
mentioned this last year,

00:38:12.184 --> 00:38:14.150
five or six years ago

00:38:14.150 --> 00:38:16.440
I tried to buy a Super Bowl ad.

00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:17.490
I tried to buy a Super Bowl ad

00:38:17.490 --> 00:38:19.140
'cause I knew nothing
about buying television

00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:21.090
and it was very expensive for a human,

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:23.450
it was basically about as much income

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:25.100
as I could've spent at
that point in my life.

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:26.220
And I was gonna run an ad

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:28.270
and it was gonna be a picture of my face,

00:38:29.490 --> 00:38:30.710
I was gonna film it on my phone

00:38:30.710 --> 00:38:32.830
and I was gonna be like, hey,
America, it's me, it's Gary,

00:38:32.830 --> 00:38:37.050
follow me on Twitter,
Gary V-E-E and be out.

00:38:37.050 --> 00:38:40.360
And I remember going through
the logic of that as a human

00:38:40.360 --> 00:38:42.570
and saying this is gonna
be the best thing I ever do

00:38:42.570 --> 00:38:43.830
because it's going to work.

00:38:43.830 --> 00:38:46.530
I'm gonna amortize out
this attention and value

00:38:46.530 --> 00:38:47.840
over the rest of my life.

00:38:47.840 --> 00:38:50.760
I think that the attention
is so underpriced

00:38:50.760 --> 00:38:53.850
in comparison to every
other option in marketing

00:38:53.850 --> 00:38:57.360
that every business should
challenge itself deeply

00:38:57.360 --> 00:38:58.230
to try to figure out,

00:38:58.230 --> 00:38:59.140
how 'bout just using it

00:38:59.140 --> 00:39:01.650
as a first party data collector?

00:39:01.650 --> 00:39:03.135
And I'm passionate about that.

00:39:03.135 --> 00:39:07.700
- While I love your
passion for the Super Bowl,

00:39:07.700 --> 00:39:11.510
I would have to argue
so much about television

00:39:11.510 --> 00:39:12.820
not being impactful

00:39:12.820 --> 00:39:15.240
because we have so much research

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:17.163
that absolutely proves the opposite

00:39:17.163 --> 00:39:19.320
including our biometrics

00:39:19.320 --> 00:39:20.670
that show how we can drive

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:21.823
any KPI.
- Don't believe.

00:39:23.560 --> 00:39:24.800
Not a buyer.

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:26.563
- Well, thank God.

00:39:29.645 --> 00:39:31.930
Or we could have that
conversation another time.

00:39:31.930 --> 00:39:32.763
- Or here?

00:39:34.895 --> 00:39:37.760
I think when people spend
other people's money,

00:39:37.760 --> 00:39:39.060
they spend it differently.

00:39:40.030 --> 00:39:43.713
And so I think that I have empathy

00:39:43.713 --> 00:39:46.650
but I'm always curious
what a lot of media buyers

00:39:46.650 --> 00:39:47.600
and executives would do

00:39:47.600 --> 00:39:49.840
if their actual children's health

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:51.290
depended on that media spend

00:39:51.290 --> 00:39:53.213
versus what they do for a living.

00:39:54.233 --> 00:39:57.120
- I think marketers
are trying to find ways

00:39:57.120 --> 00:39:59.110
to be more engaging with consumers,

00:39:59.110 --> 00:40:02.590
using the medium in a more
powerful and impactful way,

00:40:02.590 --> 00:40:04.248
content and context,

00:40:04.248 --> 00:40:08.770
putting yourself around brands
that really marry your brand

00:40:08.770 --> 00:40:11.090
to a content brand.

00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:11.923
Stacy, do you want to say something?

00:40:11.923 --> 00:40:13.987
- Yeah, so we've done on Bubly,

00:40:13.987 --> 00:40:15.320
outside of the Super Bowl,

00:40:15.320 --> 00:40:16.790
the two big investments we've had

00:40:16.790 --> 00:40:19.130
are on the television
side, are integrations,

00:40:19.130 --> 00:40:19.963
media integrations.

00:40:19.963 --> 00:40:22.680
So we have a big partnership
with the Ellen Show

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:25.040
and we've had an (mumbles)
and it's in show,

00:40:25.040 --> 00:40:27.166
all of the content is in show.
- Love, love, love.

00:40:27.166 --> 00:40:29.910
- Yeah 'cause to your point,

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:31.210
people are watching that--

00:40:33.101 --> 00:40:35.170
- Love it, that's where
television started.

00:40:35.170 --> 00:40:38.290
Ed Sullivan was brought to you
by a local town car Company.

00:40:38.290 --> 00:40:40.500
People watch things,

00:40:40.500 --> 00:40:41.850
the format of a commercial,

00:40:41.850 --> 00:40:43.450
one of my favorite things that's happening

00:40:43.450 --> 00:40:44.690
in marketing in the last five years

00:40:44.690 --> 00:40:46.960
back to your point countering my point

00:40:46.960 --> 00:40:48.640
is the way that television now with sports

00:40:48.640 --> 00:40:50.425
is splitting the screen.

00:40:50.425 --> 00:40:51.523
That's been a huge coup.

00:40:51.523 --> 00:40:54.066
I believe that's a good move.

00:40:54.066 --> 00:40:55.580
- Picture in picture is a huge thing.

00:40:55.580 --> 00:40:57.370
Now even in entertainment.

00:40:57.370 --> 00:40:59.950
And we're able to do actually
interactive picture in picture

00:40:59.950 --> 00:41:02.010
and consumers are telling
us they like that.

00:41:02.010 --> 00:41:03.190
We're seeing it in red carpet.

00:41:03.190 --> 00:41:06.330
They want to see the behind
the scenes of what's going on

00:41:06.330 --> 00:41:08.660
and you're right, we
all have to get better

00:41:08.660 --> 00:41:11.140
at improving the commercial experience

00:41:11.140 --> 00:41:12.630
and things like you're doing with Ellen

00:41:12.630 --> 00:41:14.255
and integration opportunities

00:41:14.255 --> 00:41:16.720
are huge and important.

00:41:16.720 --> 00:41:19.410
And then there's things
like the Super Bowl

00:41:19.410 --> 00:41:21.810
or the Academy Awards or the Grammy's

00:41:21.810 --> 00:41:23.360
that are gonna drive huge awareness

00:41:23.360 --> 00:41:25.220
and are around passion points

00:41:25.220 --> 00:41:26.500
that consumers love

00:41:26.500 --> 00:41:29.770
and whether it's like
fashion of the red carpet

00:41:29.770 --> 00:41:31.130
around the Academy Awards

00:41:31.130 --> 00:41:32.946
or music in the Grammy's

00:41:32.946 --> 00:41:35.360
or the whole pomp and circumstance

00:41:35.360 --> 00:41:36.983
around America and the Super Bowl.

00:41:36.983 --> 00:41:38.790
- I think we should talk about things

00:41:38.790 --> 00:41:41.190
like Instagram and Twitter's usage

00:41:41.190 --> 00:41:42.730
during sports and the Grammy's

00:41:42.730 --> 00:41:44.690
explodes during commercial time

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:46.498
as a real data point.

00:41:46.498 --> 00:41:47.904
To your point,

00:41:47.904 --> 00:41:49.930
listen, people have businesses

00:41:49.930 --> 00:41:50.763
and they're driving their thing

00:41:50.763 --> 00:41:52.160
and I have empathy for that

00:41:52.160 --> 00:41:54.570
but this industry's really silly

00:41:54.570 --> 00:41:57.050
when it talks about consumer
centric 'cause it's not.

00:41:57.050 --> 00:41:59.930
It's business centric to the
KPI's of their businesses.

00:41:59.930 --> 00:42:02.060
And I think a lot of that
creates vulnerabilities

00:42:02.060 --> 00:42:03.340
that don't need to be there.

00:42:03.340 --> 00:42:04.810
And I think if anything

00:42:04.810 --> 00:42:07.870
things like Blockbuster
going out of business

00:42:07.870 --> 00:42:10.330
and Netflix and Sears and Toys'R'Us,

00:42:10.330 --> 00:42:11.580
there's so many indicators

00:42:11.580 --> 00:42:12.780
that when you start doing things

00:42:12.780 --> 00:42:15.659
that are in your best interest
in the short term financially

00:42:15.659 --> 00:42:17.800
that are against what's
in the best interest

00:42:17.800 --> 00:42:18.633
of the consumer,

00:42:18.633 --> 00:42:20.730
that eventually becomes a losing formula.

00:42:20.730 --> 00:42:23.150
And I do think a lot of
innovation on the TV side

00:42:23.150 --> 00:42:25.850
would be a great idea sooner than later.

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:27.660
- Yeah, we're definitely seeing that.

00:42:27.660 --> 00:42:29.510
We're testing new things at NBC Universal.

00:42:29.510 --> 00:42:32.760
You have to do that to engage consumers.

00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:37.420
So anyone want to say one final point

00:42:37.420 --> 00:42:40.513
before I toss it to the audience for Q&amp;A?

00:42:41.460 --> 00:42:44.433
Super Bowl ads, one final point, Gary.

00:42:45.450 --> 00:42:46.283
- Buy them.

00:42:47.970 --> 00:42:50.631
- And spend $10 million on 30 seconds.

00:42:50.631 --> 00:42:52.048
- I believe that.

00:42:53.560 --> 00:42:56.403
- Make sure that it's the
right choice for your brand.

00:42:57.890 --> 00:42:59.910
- Yeah that was gonna be
mine and creative matters.

00:42:59.910 --> 00:43:01.553
Creative quality matters.

00:43:02.940 --> 00:43:03.773
- Okay.

00:43:07.720 --> 00:43:08.836
Yes, question?

00:43:08.836 --> 00:43:10.463
- Yeah, well repeat it.

00:43:11.710 --> 00:43:13.290
- [Male] I'm loud enough.

00:43:13.290 --> 00:43:14.716
- We can throw the mic at you too.

00:43:14.716 --> 00:43:17.390
- [Male] I guess the most
important thing that you're saying

00:43:17.390 --> 00:43:20.270
is that you have to know
what you're doing the ad for,

00:43:20.270 --> 00:43:21.610
what your goals are.

00:43:21.610 --> 00:43:25.310
Because is the only goal to sell product

00:43:25.310 --> 00:43:26.747
or is there a longer term goal

00:43:26.747 --> 00:43:29.180
which as you were pointing out Gary

00:43:29.180 --> 00:43:30.885
that there's an interplay between

00:43:30.885 --> 00:43:33.280
for better or for worse with social media

00:43:33.280 --> 00:43:34.810
that is gonna have to resonate

00:43:34.810 --> 00:43:36.060
because people are on their cell phones

00:43:36.060 --> 00:43:37.670
as much as they're on anything else?

00:43:37.670 --> 00:43:38.503
- Well I think look,

00:43:38.503 --> 00:43:39.590
these are businesses up here

00:43:39.590 --> 00:43:41.540
and there's nothing wrong
with running a business

00:43:41.540 --> 00:43:43.170
and trying to sell product.

00:43:43.170 --> 00:43:45.140
I think the best way to sell a product

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:47.090
is they actually build a brand

00:43:47.090 --> 00:43:49.482
and not be sales centric.

00:43:49.482 --> 00:43:51.440
And so I what I think Super Bowl does

00:43:51.440 --> 00:43:54.130
is gives you that potential
to do the holy grand.

00:43:54.130 --> 00:43:55.530
The best sales people

00:43:55.530 --> 00:43:56.880
are the ones that built a brand

00:43:56.880 --> 00:43:59.090
and then let people come
to them in perpetuity.

00:43:59.090 --> 00:44:00.810
So I do think the Super Bowl offers that.

00:44:00.810 --> 00:44:02.330
So yes I think that's the goal

00:44:02.330 --> 00:44:05.690
but like obviously short
and long term goals matter

00:44:05.690 --> 00:44:08.040
and this is what's so amazing
on branding and marketing

00:44:08.040 --> 00:44:11.230
over DR selling and
intent based Google ads.

00:44:11.230 --> 00:44:12.450
You can actually build something

00:44:12.450 --> 00:44:14.009
that gives you longevity.

00:44:14.009 --> 00:44:16.637
- [Male] And in regard to the social media

00:44:16.637 --> 00:44:19.643
and the interplay between
that and the social media?

00:44:19.643 --> 00:44:21.219
Does anybody else have a comment

00:44:21.219 --> 00:44:22.597
or any thought on that?

00:44:22.597 --> 00:44:24.047
- I mean look, I actually am,

00:44:24.889 --> 00:44:26.603
when I was saying that
you should accompany that

00:44:26.603 --> 00:44:28.124
with other digital assets,

00:44:28.124 --> 00:44:29.740
look, social media's great.

00:44:29.740 --> 00:44:32.383
I think it creates a lot of noise.

00:44:32.383 --> 00:44:36.080
I don't love social media from
a data collection standpoint,

00:44:36.080 --> 00:44:36.913
no one does.

00:44:36.913 --> 00:44:39.627
There's not a lot of room to
truly understand your audience

00:44:39.627 --> 00:44:41.760
or to be able to harness that data

00:44:41.760 --> 00:44:43.830
in a strategic way for your brand.

00:44:43.830 --> 00:44:48.130
So I think it's great to think
about the higher level stuff

00:44:48.130 --> 00:44:51.121
and then with the kind of
brand awareness campaigns.

00:44:51.121 --> 00:44:53.560
But I think fundamentally
you need to figure out a way

00:44:53.560 --> 00:44:56.300
to drive people back
to your own properties.

00:44:56.300 --> 00:44:57.420
You have to figure out

00:44:57.420 --> 00:44:59.450
how to have a direct
conversation with your audience.

00:44:59.450 --> 00:45:00.730
And that's really the only way

00:45:00.730 --> 00:45:02.593
you can start driving towards sales.

00:45:03.940 --> 00:45:05.070
At least in our world,

00:45:05.070 --> 00:45:06.473
in the B2B world.

00:45:08.727 --> 00:45:10.879
- [Lee] Hi, my name's Lee Nabidor.

00:45:10.879 --> 00:45:12.100
I'd like to spur this discussion.

00:45:12.100 --> 00:45:15.190
Let's assume yes, Super Bowl, okay.

00:45:15.190 --> 00:45:18.137
One of the things that I
thought was really interesting

00:45:18.137 --> 00:45:21.593
and I know it's a word that's
thrown around is authenticity.

00:45:22.820 --> 00:45:24.630
Forget the celebrity for a second.

00:45:24.630 --> 00:45:27.320
Let's talk about actually entertainment

00:45:27.320 --> 00:45:29.830
versus product explanation.

00:45:29.830 --> 00:45:31.870
I thought Bud Light did
some interesting things.

00:45:31.870 --> 00:45:32.703
One was up there

00:45:32.703 --> 00:45:34.040
but the other ones that were up there

00:45:34.040 --> 00:45:36.620
with the corn syrup and so forth

00:45:36.620 --> 00:45:38.650
and trying to do it in an entertaining way

00:45:38.650 --> 00:45:41.610
but actually making a
point about the product.

00:45:41.610 --> 00:45:45.540
But two years ago I orchestrated
a campaign for Mini Cooper

00:45:45.540 --> 00:45:46.757
called Define Their Goals

00:45:46.757 --> 00:45:49.180
and it was basically a program

00:45:49.180 --> 00:45:51.472
that had celebrities, was entertaining

00:45:51.472 --> 00:45:53.890
but when we looked in the mirror and said

00:45:53.890 --> 00:45:56.310
what is it that people are
saying about our product

00:45:56.310 --> 00:45:57.817
and how do we defy that

00:45:57.817 --> 00:46:01.760
with celebrities who
also define that label

00:46:01.760 --> 00:46:03.280
and have a mini?

00:46:03.280 --> 00:46:05.160
My question really is,

00:46:05.160 --> 00:46:06.460
and I don't think we touched on it,

00:46:06.460 --> 00:46:08.733
when you look at something
like Gillette, okay,

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:11.270
the criticism that I heard

00:46:11.270 --> 00:46:13.470
and I could be debated either way

00:46:13.470 --> 00:46:16.900
and I'd love to hear from
you guys on authenticity.

00:46:16.900 --> 00:46:19.160
What was the problem that they had

00:46:19.160 --> 00:46:21.491
by either having the conversation

00:46:21.491 --> 00:46:26.390
or did they not have an authentic thing

00:46:26.390 --> 00:46:29.040
beyond just putting the
conversation out there?

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:30.900
And I think it's really
interesting to look at

00:46:30.900 --> 00:46:32.750
when you're gonna make a big statement,

00:46:32.750 --> 00:46:35.390
it's easy to say we don't
have corn syrup and all that

00:46:35.390 --> 00:46:37.280
but if Gillette is gonna say

00:46:38.290 --> 00:46:40.380
we're the best a man could be

00:46:40.380 --> 00:46:43.160
and we're gonna do this
and support women's issues,

00:46:43.160 --> 00:46:46.370
and then they don't actually
support the women's issues

00:46:46.370 --> 00:46:47.920
that becomes a problem.

00:46:47.920 --> 00:46:50.340
And I just want to ask I guess the group

00:46:50.340 --> 00:46:53.150
about brands making statements

00:46:53.150 --> 00:46:57.630
that are in the social discussion

00:46:57.630 --> 00:46:59.470
and how authentic do you have to be

00:46:59.470 --> 00:47:01.853
to get into that kind of discussion?

00:47:01.853 --> 00:47:04.160
- So I would say Proctor and Gamble

00:47:04.160 --> 00:47:06.640
is actually putting their
money where their mouth is

00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:07.916
and are leading the charge

00:47:07.916 --> 00:47:10.470
with changing the way
that women are portrayed

00:47:10.470 --> 00:47:12.743
in media and advertising,

00:47:12.743 --> 00:47:15.154
with #seeher, right?

00:47:15.154 --> 00:47:16.910
- [Male] Yeah see her.

00:47:16.910 --> 00:47:20.120
- So I think it could've been, right,

00:47:20.120 --> 00:47:21.480
that could've been a miss

00:47:21.480 --> 00:47:22.950
because people don't know that

00:47:22.950 --> 00:47:24.710
if you're not in our business.

00:47:24.710 --> 00:47:26.870
And how do you then attach

00:47:26.870 --> 00:47:29.940
so that consumers know
that you are doing good

00:47:29.940 --> 00:47:33.690
and it is tough I think to link together

00:47:33.690 --> 00:47:34.580
what you're doing

00:47:34.580 --> 00:47:38.210
versus making a big, bold
statement that they made.

00:47:38.210 --> 00:47:39.043
And I could see

00:47:39.043 --> 00:47:41.926
that there could be a
disconnect for some people

00:47:41.926 --> 00:47:43.650
when they watch that ad.

00:47:43.650 --> 00:47:44.730
- [Lee] More pointed,

00:47:44.730 --> 00:47:46.070
whether Gillette or the others,

00:47:46.070 --> 00:47:49.810
just people are going
and they're going deeper.

00:47:49.810 --> 00:47:50.874
They're going onto social,

00:47:50.874 --> 00:47:53.970
they're doing research on the brand

00:47:53.970 --> 00:47:57.520
and what's the barometer for
we're gonna make this statement

00:47:57.520 --> 00:47:59.800
and here's our backup for it's real

00:47:59.800 --> 00:48:03.870
versus we're gonna talk
in a social conversation

00:48:03.870 --> 00:48:05.230
and just have the discussion.

00:48:05.230 --> 00:48:07.351
But there's nothing deeper.

00:48:07.351 --> 00:48:08.770
- I think you know the answer.

00:48:08.770 --> 00:48:09.940
Nobody likes somebody

00:48:09.940 --> 00:48:11.500
who's all talk and no action.

00:48:11.500 --> 00:48:13.380
That's just something we don't appreciate.

00:48:13.380 --> 00:48:14.940
Hypocrisy's not cool.

00:48:14.940 --> 00:48:17.803
And so I think yes, it's a really bad idea

00:48:17.803 --> 00:48:20.310
to try to look the part

00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:21.240
and not back it up

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:23.890
because people are looking
for that vulnerability.

00:48:23.890 --> 00:48:27.705
Our judgment levels on each
other and businesses today

00:48:27.705 --> 00:48:29.480
are enormously high

00:48:29.480 --> 00:48:32.580
and so yeah, that's
always been a bad idea.

00:48:32.580 --> 00:48:35.280
That's no different than
your product not being great.

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:37.500
So that's a different version

00:48:37.500 --> 00:48:39.660
of your product not being great.

00:48:39.660 --> 00:48:40.860
If you're gonna make the statement,

00:48:40.860 --> 00:48:42.010
you've got to back it up.

00:48:42.010 --> 00:48:46.600
And America is an interesting place.

00:48:46.600 --> 00:48:49.420
We don't often mind the crime.

00:48:49.420 --> 00:48:52.990
We mind when the person's
trying to cover it up.

00:48:52.990 --> 00:48:54.390
We don't like to be fooled.

00:48:54.390 --> 00:48:56.088
And so yeah if a company comes out

00:48:56.088 --> 00:48:57.900
and they're trying to make a big to do

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:00.450
but then their actions
don't speak to that,

00:49:00.450 --> 00:49:01.700
but that's happening every day.

00:49:01.700 --> 00:49:03.090
You look on the coasts,

00:49:03.090 --> 00:49:05.440
the amount of people that
talk about the environment

00:49:05.440 --> 00:49:07.020
but aren't willing to talk about

00:49:07.020 --> 00:49:09.990
that their Prada bags or
their clothes and textiles

00:49:09.990 --> 00:49:12.890
are driving some of the
biggest damage in our society,

00:49:12.890 --> 00:49:15.640
we are one to two issue people.

00:49:15.640 --> 00:49:17.480
And so like--

00:49:17.480 --> 00:49:18.680
- [Lee] That's kind of what I'm asking.

00:49:18.680 --> 00:49:19.951
How deep do you have to go?

00:49:19.951 --> 00:49:20.784
- I think it's really tricky

00:49:20.784 --> 00:49:23.950
because even if you do back
it up with authenticity

00:49:23.950 --> 00:49:25.420
and that is not easy,

00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:26.590
and then you are about that.

00:49:26.590 --> 00:49:29.010
And so even if you do it all right,

00:49:29.010 --> 00:49:31.140
is it the right thing
to be about that issue?

00:49:31.140 --> 00:49:33.973
You have to decide that
as a for-profit business.

00:49:33.973 --> 00:49:36.240
And I do think having
a purpose in the world

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:37.940
is something that every
brand needs to have.

00:49:37.940 --> 00:49:41.730
But tying that purpose to
a very hot social issue,

00:49:41.730 --> 00:49:43.310
I think each brand needs to decide

00:49:43.310 --> 00:49:45.050
if that's worth the risk.

00:49:45.050 --> 00:49:45.883
By the way, did everybody see

00:49:45.883 --> 00:49:49.810
the Miller New York Times ad
today responding to Bud Light?

00:49:49.810 --> 00:49:51.869
That was awesome.

00:49:51.869 --> 00:49:53.450
- Is it ever a good idea

00:49:53.450 --> 00:49:56.238
to put somebody down during that?

00:49:56.238 --> 00:49:59.364
- I think Miller responded great to that.

00:49:59.364 --> 00:50:01.740
- I think the amount of people
that saw Miller's response

00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:03.460
versus Bud's move

00:50:03.460 --> 00:50:04.850
is an enormous delta

00:50:04.850 --> 00:50:07.646
and I think that's an
important thing for me.

00:50:07.646 --> 00:50:10.580
- But they could've done that
ad without actually calling--

00:50:10.580 --> 00:50:12.490
- I loved it.

00:50:12.490 --> 00:50:14.812
I'll be on the record saying I love it.

00:50:14.812 --> 00:50:17.320
I think businesses need to
do a little bit more of that.

00:50:17.320 --> 00:50:19.350
I think it's a very poignant point.

00:50:19.350 --> 00:50:20.183
It caught your attention

00:50:20.183 --> 00:50:21.820
that they were willing to even go there.

00:50:21.820 --> 00:50:24.750
And I think back to us being hypocrites,

00:50:24.750 --> 00:50:27.750
we don't like attack ads in politicians

00:50:27.750 --> 00:50:30.090
yet it's very clear that it works

00:50:30.090 --> 00:50:33.960
and so I think what I appreciated

00:50:33.960 --> 00:50:36.000
was it was an attack

00:50:36.000 --> 00:50:38.380
in a very, kind of softer way.

00:50:38.380 --> 00:50:39.230
I thought it was clever

00:50:39.230 --> 00:50:41.310
and I think there's more upside.

00:50:41.310 --> 00:50:43.890
I don't know how big the
issue is for the end consumer.

00:50:43.890 --> 00:50:45.430
I'm just not educated enough on that.

00:50:45.430 --> 00:50:47.252
But it definitely got the point across.

00:50:47.252 --> 00:50:49.650
And back to why I believe
in the Super Bowl,

00:50:49.650 --> 00:50:51.310
man do I think very few people

00:50:51.310 --> 00:50:55.070
saw the print ad in the Times
today at scale in comparison.

00:50:55.070 --> 00:50:55.930
- Yeah I think that's true.

00:50:55.930 --> 00:50:57.030
It's funny your point,

00:50:58.210 --> 00:50:59.752
there was a lot of discussion,

00:50:59.752 --> 00:51:01.457
positive and negative,

00:51:01.457 --> 00:51:03.100
more positive but some negative

00:51:03.100 --> 00:51:05.460
about the Pepsi commercial
had the word coke in it

00:51:05.460 --> 00:51:06.780
and like oh my God.

00:51:06.780 --> 00:51:07.710
- I love that you did it.

00:51:07.710 --> 00:51:08.790
- Pepsi has challenged your brand.

00:51:08.790 --> 00:51:12.110
That's what Pepsi does and that is a truth

00:51:12.110 --> 00:51:14.330
that when you go and you
order a coke at a restaurant

00:51:14.330 --> 00:51:16.060
they say, is Pepsi okay?

00:51:16.060 --> 00:51:18.700
That is something that
is true in the world

00:51:18.700 --> 00:51:19.743
and we've built off of that.

00:51:19.743 --> 00:51:21.820
That said something about our brand.

00:51:21.820 --> 00:51:24.353
But not everybody is
comfortable with that.

00:51:25.259 --> 00:51:28.203
- And I think that's where we get too B2B.

00:51:29.320 --> 00:51:32.030
I don't know if the head
honchos at Pepsi heard

00:51:32.030 --> 00:51:36.330
but most of us know Coke and
Pepsi have some similarities.

00:51:36.330 --> 00:51:38.493
We're just so not consumer centric.

00:51:41.044 --> 00:51:42.386
- You have a question.

00:51:42.386 --> 00:51:43.840
- Yes.
- I'm glad you did that.

00:51:43.840 --> 00:51:45.080
- [Female] Super Bowl surrounds

00:51:45.080 --> 00:51:48.560
a halo effect strategy or campaign.

00:51:48.560 --> 00:51:52.780
So recently when I was watching
all of these ads on Monday,

00:51:52.780 --> 00:51:55.180
a GMC ad popped up

00:51:55.180 --> 00:51:59.020
on whatever website I was
watching these articles

00:51:59.020 --> 00:52:03.250
and the title card came in
saying the Rams already lost,

00:52:03.250 --> 00:52:04.760
went into selling GMC ad.

00:52:04.760 --> 00:52:07.040
So super fast, super relevant.

00:52:07.040 --> 00:52:08.760
They were prepared for it.

00:52:08.760 --> 00:52:10.330
Had maybe two weeks notice

00:52:10.330 --> 00:52:12.630
as to who was gonna do it.

00:52:12.630 --> 00:52:15.529
I don't feel I'm ever pitched as a client.

00:52:15.529 --> 00:52:17.420
What should my surround sound

00:52:17.420 --> 00:52:19.653
of an event that could affect me be.

00:52:20.640 --> 00:52:23.460
And so is that a more cost effective way

00:52:23.460 --> 00:52:24.760
to leverage the Super Bowl

00:52:24.760 --> 00:52:28.543
if I can't do a five to 10
million dollar 30 second spot?

00:52:31.360 --> 00:52:33.410
- I mean if you can't do the spot,

00:52:33.410 --> 00:52:35.040
you've got to do what you can do about it.

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:36.690
I think one of the reasons a lot of times

00:52:36.690 --> 00:52:38.700
there aren't good holistic,

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:40.350
I used to always wonder
when I first got in,

00:52:40.350 --> 00:52:42.790
why aren't people a little
more 360 with their campaigns?

00:52:42.790 --> 00:52:44.500
And that was because I didn't understand

00:52:44.500 --> 00:52:46.960
the dynamics of the
creative agency landscape.

00:52:46.960 --> 00:52:48.950
I didn't know that creative agencies

00:52:48.950 --> 00:52:50.790
thought everything besides TVC

00:52:50.790 --> 00:52:52.340
was bullshit and garbage

00:52:52.340 --> 00:52:54.150
and not of respect

00:52:54.150 --> 00:52:56.490
and didn't mean anything
and after thoughts.

00:52:56.490 --> 00:52:58.610
And so once I learned that,

00:52:58.610 --> 00:52:59.443
then I understood.

00:52:59.443 --> 00:53:00.276
I'm like oh okay

00:53:00.276 --> 00:53:02.080
they just completely disrespected,

00:53:02.080 --> 00:53:03.540
put no energy to it.

00:53:03.540 --> 00:53:06.054
Plus then media's the
separate thing to begin with.

00:53:06.054 --> 00:53:10.200
And the KPIs and we're not
gonna buy that inventory

00:53:10.200 --> 00:53:12.510
'cause it doesn't achieve the low cost CPM

00:53:12.510 --> 00:53:13.747
that we have to hit to get bonused.

00:53:13.747 --> 00:53:15.890
And so real life takes over.

00:53:15.890 --> 00:53:18.093
But that's a good idea.

00:53:20.335 --> 00:53:22.010
And that's the punch line.
- There's a question

00:53:22.010 --> 00:53:22.980
in the back I think.

00:53:22.980 --> 00:53:25.112
- The right things aren't being done

00:53:25.112 --> 00:53:28.150
because of the vested interest of partners

00:53:28.150 --> 00:53:30.450
within the reality of this business world.

00:53:30.450 --> 00:53:32.080
- So sorry as a follow-up,

00:53:32.080 --> 00:53:35.210
is the solution then for brands like GPMC

00:53:35.210 --> 00:53:37.520
to in-house more of their work?

00:53:37.520 --> 00:53:39.120
- That sounds like a great idea.

00:53:41.771 --> 00:53:43.146
I'm in disproportionate belief

00:53:43.146 --> 00:53:44.010
that every brand in the world

00:53:44.010 --> 00:53:45.460
should do all their marketing in-house

00:53:45.460 --> 00:53:46.560
if they're capable to.

00:53:49.840 --> 00:53:52.120
Being dead fucking serious.

00:53:52.120 --> 00:53:53.190
The problem is--

00:53:53.190 --> 00:53:56.663
- I was really hoping I'd
catch you with that one.

00:53:57.830 --> 00:54:02.048
- I will never, ever put my
financial vested interest

00:54:02.048 --> 00:54:04.330
in front of being historically correct

00:54:04.330 --> 00:54:06.580
'cause that's where I
think people struggle

00:54:06.580 --> 00:54:07.894
and make big mistakes.

00:54:07.894 --> 00:54:10.050
The reality is if you're capable,

00:54:10.050 --> 00:54:11.036
the problem for brands

00:54:11.036 --> 00:54:12.210
when you play it out

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:14.260
is when the economy gets soft

00:54:14.260 --> 00:54:16.550
and Wall Street doesn't
like all that overhead,

00:54:16.550 --> 00:54:17.670
it helps to have,

00:54:17.670 --> 00:54:19.100
so there's all these real things

00:54:19.100 --> 00:54:22.730
but yes, I think anything
you can do better

00:54:22.730 --> 00:54:26.173
for a better price with
better intent is a good idea.

00:54:27.510 --> 00:54:28.380
- Question in the back.

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:29.330
Two more questions.

00:54:31.426 --> 00:54:33.753
- [Male] Hi, quick question.

00:54:33.753 --> 00:54:35.754
With digital marketing right--

00:54:35.754 --> 00:54:37.797
- Can you hold your mic closer?

00:54:37.797 --> 00:54:39.505
- [Male] Sorry, can you hear me?

00:54:39.505 --> 00:54:43.600
In digital marketing, we
see a lot of personalized,

00:54:43.600 --> 00:54:48.410
hypo targeted assets,
right, hitting consumers.

00:54:48.410 --> 00:54:52.553
So my question is what's
going to happen to this,

00:54:53.418 --> 00:54:56.140
to advertising in general
for the Super Bowl

00:54:56.140 --> 00:55:00.740
when a streaming service
such as Netflix or Amazon

00:55:00.740 --> 00:55:03.350
gets on the rights?

00:55:03.350 --> 00:55:08.141
Because yes, we have CBS,
we have NBC Universal,

00:55:08.141 --> 00:55:10.660
getting the Super Bowl

00:55:10.660 --> 00:55:12.580
and they're investing
into streaming services.

00:55:12.580 --> 00:55:15.790
But what happens when the
non-traditional streaming service

00:55:15.790 --> 00:55:17.163
such as Netflix, Amazon,

00:55:18.280 --> 00:55:21.640
gets their hands on the Super Bowl

00:55:21.640 --> 00:55:25.323
in terms of how will that affect metrics?

00:55:26.493 --> 00:55:28.100
How will that affect media buys?

00:55:28.100 --> 00:55:30.620
How will that affect content in general?

00:55:30.620 --> 00:55:32.830
Are we gonna see personalized content

00:55:32.830 --> 00:55:37.010
or are we gonna see these
wide net advertising baits?

00:55:37.010 --> 00:55:38.999
- So Gary said it before

00:55:38.999 --> 00:55:43.160
in terms of we have to look
where the consumer is going

00:55:43.160 --> 00:55:45.730
and we all need to work
together as an industry

00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:47.618
to really change the ecosystem

00:55:47.618 --> 00:55:49.590
of how advertising works

00:55:49.590 --> 00:55:51.900
in the places that it's available.

00:55:51.900 --> 00:55:55.100
And you also said you go back
to the day to day realities

00:55:55.100 --> 00:55:59.295
of like I need to make
my OCF and hit my DNLs

00:55:59.295 --> 00:56:01.020
and the reality set in

00:56:01.020 --> 00:56:03.220
but the consumers aren't gonna stop moving

00:56:03.220 --> 00:56:04.396
the way that they're moving.

00:56:04.396 --> 00:56:09.183
And so look, there were pictures of Bezos

00:56:09.183 --> 00:56:11.720
with the head of the NFL

00:56:12.610 --> 00:56:15.220
and that's something that could be scary.

00:56:15.220 --> 00:56:19.770
But can they really continue
to be non-profitable

00:56:20.910 --> 00:56:23.500
and make money just on subscriptions?

00:56:23.500 --> 00:56:25.980
Because a one way revenue stream

00:56:25.980 --> 00:56:27.990
has proven that it will fail

00:56:27.990 --> 00:56:30.030
and that you need dual revenue stream

00:56:30.030 --> 00:56:32.010
with the price of content.

00:56:32.010 --> 00:56:33.810
And you see someone like Facebook

00:56:33.810 --> 00:56:36.530
who's unwilling to get
into the content game

00:56:36.530 --> 00:56:39.120
because their margins
would go through the floor.

00:56:39.120 --> 00:56:41.750
So it's really us working together

00:56:41.750 --> 00:56:43.370
I think as an industry

00:56:43.370 --> 00:56:46.340
to give consumers advertising.

00:56:46.340 --> 00:56:50.190
Because three out of four
people who watch the Super Bowl

00:56:50.190 --> 00:56:51.023
said that they were

00:56:51.023 --> 00:56:52.820
looking forward to seeing the commercials.

00:56:52.820 --> 00:56:55.330
It is part of the pop culture conversation

00:56:55.330 --> 00:56:56.820
and they want creative

00:56:56.820 --> 00:57:00.090
and they also want to be
informed about new brands.

00:57:00.090 --> 00:57:02.443
So I really think it's working together

00:57:02.443 --> 00:57:03.810
to make it better.

00:57:03.810 --> 00:57:07.390
- My take on that is three
out of four sounds right

00:57:07.390 --> 00:57:09.140
and low intuitively

00:57:09.140 --> 00:57:11.050
but what about every other commercial

00:57:11.050 --> 00:57:12.520
that exists in the world?

00:57:12.520 --> 00:57:13.880
What's the polling on that?

00:57:13.880 --> 00:57:16.960
And when Amazon gets the NFL rights,

00:57:16.960 --> 00:57:20.288
it's gonna come down to how
they want to monetize it.

00:57:20.288 --> 00:57:21.560
To your point,

00:57:21.560 --> 00:57:24.900
how long will Wall Street
allow them to invest

00:57:24.900 --> 00:57:27.050
and when does the bloom come off the rose

00:57:27.050 --> 00:57:28.280
and that becomes the question.

00:57:28.280 --> 00:57:31.210
But once you have that much
first party data and behavior,

00:57:31.210 --> 00:57:32.043
nobody here,

00:57:32.043 --> 00:57:35.300
I ask everybody here to ask themselves

00:57:35.300 --> 00:57:37.320
how much they're willing
to pay for Amazon Prime

00:57:37.320 --> 00:57:38.960
versus what they're paying now.

00:57:38.960 --> 00:57:43.660
- Well Hulu, when they offered
the 99 cents for ad load,

00:57:43.660 --> 00:57:48.140
most of the subscribers took the ad load.

00:57:48.140 --> 00:57:50.802
So they couldn't--
- 'Cause Hulu's

00:57:50.802 --> 00:57:52.840
one dimensional.

00:57:52.840 --> 00:57:54.850
Hulu's one dimensional.

00:57:54.850 --> 00:57:55.800
One more time,

00:57:55.800 --> 00:57:58.370
how much is everybody willing
to pay for Amazon Prime

00:57:58.370 --> 00:57:59.203
which delivers

00:57:59.203 --> 00:58:00.246
all your products?
- I actually think

00:58:00.246 --> 00:58:01.079
Amazon Prime is not as good as Hulu

00:58:01.079 --> 00:58:02.680
and I think consumers in general--

00:58:02.680 --> 00:58:04.330
- I'm talking about actual Amazon Prime.

00:58:04.330 --> 00:58:05.770
The shit that everybody here has

00:58:05.770 --> 00:58:07.426
to buy everything they buy.

00:58:07.426 --> 00:58:10.952
- So whatever they charge is the answer.

00:58:10.952 --> 00:58:14.680
- What we're having is we're
having silo conversations.

00:58:14.680 --> 00:58:16.300
And when Amazon has last mile

00:58:16.300 --> 00:58:17.400
and you can order toothpaste

00:58:17.400 --> 00:58:21.030
and get it in 24 seconds in your door,

00:58:21.030 --> 00:58:22.950
I think we're having a naive conversation

00:58:22.950 --> 00:58:24.850
and when the entire, guys,

00:58:24.850 --> 00:58:26.165
if the terrorists

00:58:26.165 --> 00:58:29.020
attacked Amazon's
structure for the internet,

00:58:29.020 --> 00:58:30.370
the whole internet would collapse.

00:58:30.370 --> 00:58:32.540
Everybody is on the infrastructure.

00:58:32.540 --> 00:58:36.888
So there is a lot of leverage
points that Amazon has

00:58:36.888 --> 00:58:39.330
that we have to factor into this

00:58:39.330 --> 00:58:40.650
because they have leverage.

00:58:40.650 --> 00:58:42.810
Everybody here would
pay a lot more for Prime

00:58:42.810 --> 00:58:45.330
'cause the value of free delivery

00:58:45.330 --> 00:58:47.370
across all the products
and services they buy

00:58:47.370 --> 00:58:48.530
is enormous

00:58:48.530 --> 00:58:50.257
and that's just real life.

00:58:50.257 --> 00:58:51.497
And it would be interesting to see,

00:58:51.497 --> 00:58:52.730
and I don't know,

00:58:52.730 --> 00:58:56.010
how much more are we willing
to pay for that core service

00:58:56.010 --> 00:58:57.024
that is so important?

00:58:57.024 --> 00:58:59.293
They layer on top of it.

00:59:00.330 --> 00:59:04.450
I mean they have a lot of leverage.

00:59:04.450 --> 00:59:05.823
- I think to your question,

00:59:05.823 --> 00:59:08.070
it just means that we're all in this room

00:59:08.070 --> 00:59:10.190
gonna have to get a
lot smarter and better.

00:59:10.190 --> 00:59:12.960
And it doesn't mean that
we're gonna stop advertising

00:59:12.960 --> 00:59:13.867
and brand building

00:59:13.867 --> 00:59:15.830
but we're gonna have to
do it in a different way

00:59:15.830 --> 00:59:16.700
and if the Super Bowl's

00:59:16.700 --> 00:59:18.460
no longer the appetizing cultural event,

00:59:18.460 --> 00:59:20.148
we'll find another way to do it.

00:59:20.148 --> 00:59:20.981
- And we did it.

00:59:20.981 --> 00:59:23.051
Super Bowl one wasn't even sold out.

00:59:23.051 --> 00:59:26.130
The ads on Joe Lewis's fight on the radio

00:59:26.130 --> 00:59:27.870
were a fortune in the '40s.

00:59:27.870 --> 00:59:29.240
This is not that long ago.

00:59:29.240 --> 00:59:31.270
We're always gonna move
to where the attention is.

00:59:31.270 --> 00:59:33.630
The question becomes the ad formats.

00:59:33.630 --> 00:59:35.320
The reason I love the Ellen integration

00:59:35.320 --> 00:59:36.670
is that's good.

00:59:36.670 --> 00:59:38.300
You're consuming that.

00:59:38.300 --> 00:59:40.150
I think brands should be spending way more

00:59:40.150 --> 00:59:42.870
integrating into the actual content

00:59:42.870 --> 00:59:44.530
and how networks decide to do that

00:59:44.530 --> 00:59:45.790
and things of that nature.

00:59:45.790 --> 00:59:46.623
Amazon might come out

00:59:46.623 --> 00:59:48.560
and not have commercials or who knows?

00:59:48.560 --> 00:59:50.750
But what Amazon has is something

00:59:50.750 --> 00:59:52.230
that nobody else has.

00:59:52.230 --> 00:59:53.730
When they have the NFL rights

00:59:53.730 --> 00:59:55.310
and you're watching your game

00:59:55.310 --> 00:59:58.390
and a player scores a
touchdown and you're excited

00:59:58.390 --> 01:00:00.910
and the friction of you being able to know

01:00:00.910 --> 01:00:03.240
that because something just
happened during their game

01:00:03.240 --> 01:00:06.490
that if you just say, Alexa,
order me a Sam Donald jersey,

01:00:06.490 --> 01:00:08.470
it's discounted 'cause they're playing.

01:00:08.470 --> 01:00:10.360
They're eliminating friction.

01:00:10.360 --> 01:00:11.193
They're gonna make revenue

01:00:11.193 --> 01:00:13.385
in a way other people haven't.

01:00:13.385 --> 01:00:14.600
- [Male] You'll replay it on Twitch.

01:00:14.600 --> 01:00:16.230
- Yeah but do you know what I mean?

01:00:16.230 --> 01:00:17.063
They're eliminating friction.

01:00:17.063 --> 01:00:18.440
You're watching a football game,

01:00:18.440 --> 01:00:20.500
something good happens,
you're a diehard fan

01:00:20.500 --> 01:00:22.870
and you're aware now through
marketing and branding

01:00:22.870 --> 01:00:26.330
that you can just buy that
product through voice.

01:00:26.330 --> 01:00:27.660
What we do all the time

01:00:27.660 --> 01:00:29.240
is we make binary decisions

01:00:29.240 --> 01:00:31.410
without understanding the other variables

01:00:31.410 --> 01:00:33.280
that this infrastructure is creating.

01:00:33.280 --> 01:00:36.010
That's gonna make them highly profitable.

01:00:36.010 --> 01:00:37.900
They're content's valuable to them.

01:00:37.900 --> 01:00:38.974
They sell shit.

01:00:38.974 --> 01:00:40.770
NBC and ESPN don't.

01:00:40.770 --> 01:00:42.437
- I think it's really
important to also remember

01:00:42.437 --> 01:00:44.810
that a few years ago we
were talking about Facebook

01:00:44.810 --> 01:00:46.023
exactly the same way.

01:00:46.023 --> 01:00:48.510
I think there's gonna be
a lot of ethical issues

01:00:48.510 --> 01:00:49.480
that come into play

01:00:49.480 --> 01:00:51.050
that I think we should consider.

01:00:51.050 --> 01:00:53.200
This idea that Amazon's
taking over the world

01:00:53.200 --> 01:00:54.480
and they're gonna move their servers

01:00:54.480 --> 01:00:57.625
and the Alexa's gonna fully integrate

01:00:57.625 --> 01:01:01.310
and in a really creepy way
completely change our lives,

01:01:01.310 --> 01:01:02.410
I think potentially

01:01:02.410 --> 01:01:04.380
might have some checks
and balances in place.

01:01:04.380 --> 01:01:06.100
- Sure the government gets involved

01:01:06.100 --> 01:01:07.730
when somebody wins too much.

01:01:07.730 --> 01:01:09.280
- Yeah but I think it's
even more than that.

01:01:09.280 --> 01:01:11.520
I do wonder what the evolution
of Facebook is going to be

01:01:11.520 --> 01:01:12.650
in a world where the consumer

01:01:12.650 --> 01:01:14.070
is becoming a lot more vigilant

01:01:14.070 --> 01:01:15.834
about what's happening with their data.

01:01:15.834 --> 01:01:18.387
- Well if you look at
Instagram's behavior,

01:01:18.387 --> 01:01:21.530
the answer is we go where we value things.

01:01:21.530 --> 01:01:24.060
- It's a very different thing,
Instagram versus Facebook

01:01:24.060 --> 01:01:27.160
and the brand awareness
around the issues of Facebook

01:01:27.160 --> 01:01:29.940
for some reason don't
relate to Instagram for now.

01:01:29.940 --> 01:01:31.380
I think they might.

01:01:31.380 --> 01:01:32.963
- Because hypocrisy is cute.

01:01:34.050 --> 01:01:36.650
- But we want one last
question in the back, right?

01:01:36.650 --> 01:01:37.982
- [Female] Hi.

01:01:37.982 --> 01:01:40.500
So I guess it's two questions.

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:44.120
If you were to see a red wine company ad,

01:01:44.120 --> 01:01:46.400
would you have had a better
Super Bowl ad experience

01:01:46.400 --> 01:01:47.608
and how would you have felt

01:01:47.608 --> 01:01:51.443
about whatever that brand
was that showed that to you?

01:01:51.443 --> 01:01:53.580
Because I feel like that's a valid one

01:01:53.580 --> 01:01:54.690
to the original technology

01:01:54.690 --> 01:01:57.540
but I'm wondering when
it's gonna come too late

01:01:57.540 --> 01:01:59.553
because I think we're ready for it.

01:01:59.553 --> 01:02:02.590
- Well I'm just gonna really
quickly say something.

01:02:02.590 --> 01:02:05.550
I think there is an
element of me seeing that

01:02:05.550 --> 01:02:07.295
and feeling like I discovered it

01:02:07.295 --> 01:02:09.871
and there's something
really special about it

01:02:09.871 --> 01:02:12.770
versus feeling like someone's read my mind

01:02:12.770 --> 01:02:14.470
and has put that into my plate

01:02:14.470 --> 01:02:17.250
in a way that feels really
creepy and intrusive.

01:02:17.250 --> 01:02:20.580
I am one of the two to
five percent of people

01:02:20.580 --> 01:02:22.750
who actually care about how
their data is being used

01:02:22.750 --> 01:02:24.600
'cause I just know too much about it.

01:02:24.600 --> 01:02:26.750
But I would say half
the people don't care.

01:02:26.750 --> 01:02:28.060
I think that might change with time

01:02:28.060 --> 01:02:29.270
and regardless it doesn't matter

01:02:29.270 --> 01:02:31.130
because if five percent
of the people care enough

01:02:31.130 --> 01:02:33.850
they're going to change the
way everything else works.

01:02:33.850 --> 01:02:35.400
- Also consumers are moving

01:02:35.400 --> 01:02:38.913
the way that they view traditional
linear television content

01:02:38.913 --> 01:02:40.520
in a digital format

01:02:40.520 --> 01:02:45.090
whether it's a Roku or you name your app.

01:02:45.090 --> 01:02:48.260
And so those products are data enabled

01:02:48.260 --> 01:02:49.790
and you're gonna see a huge shift

01:02:49.790 --> 01:02:54.286
of being able to do
addressable linear television

01:02:54.286 --> 01:02:56.300
like you've never been able to do before.

01:02:56.300 --> 01:02:59.370
The technology is now catching
up with consumer behavior.

01:02:59.370 --> 01:03:02.313
So I think it's a really
exciting times for all of us

01:03:02.313 --> 01:03:05.580
but again, we have to protect the consumer

01:03:05.580 --> 01:03:06.814
and give them their privacy

01:03:06.814 --> 01:03:10.680
and be responsible with what we do.

01:03:10.680 --> 01:03:12.870
And I would like to add
that Jeff Bezos did say

01:03:12.870 --> 01:03:14.430
that television advertising

01:03:14.430 --> 01:03:17.000
was one of the most powerful vehicles

01:03:17.000 --> 01:03:19.760
and he wasn't a fan and changed
his mind in the last year

01:03:19.760 --> 01:03:21.077
because he started to invest heavily

01:03:21.077 --> 01:03:24.010
and see how it was driving sales

01:03:24.010 --> 01:03:26.473
in usage of Amazon Prime.

01:03:27.900 --> 01:03:32.810
- I think using fear as a
sales tactic is interesting.

01:03:32.810 --> 01:03:35.025
And I think that is an important,

01:03:35.025 --> 01:03:36.959
seriously, I think it's
a really important,

01:03:36.959 --> 01:03:38.310
I think it's interesting.

01:03:38.310 --> 01:03:41.180
First of all, everybody just has opinions

01:03:41.180 --> 01:03:42.280
and then things play out

01:03:42.280 --> 01:03:45.570
and I value massively

01:03:45.570 --> 01:03:46.440
people that have different

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:49.020
points of view on things, massively.

01:03:49.020 --> 01:03:51.501
I think that there's
ideologies around human beings

01:03:51.501 --> 01:03:54.890
that historically humans
haven't delivered on.

01:03:54.890 --> 01:03:57.430
And so that's what I'm trading on

01:03:57.430 --> 01:03:59.730
but this may be the singular
issue and that's fine

01:03:59.730 --> 01:04:02.733
and I think that's great.

01:04:04.640 --> 01:04:06.670
- Well I think that wraps up our show.

01:04:06.670 --> 01:04:08.701
Thank you Knotch for having us today.

01:04:08.701 --> 01:04:11.701
(audience applause)

